tel 1.0

base ultimatum
import soundness

info   terminal user interfaces: a solved pane layout, interactive forms, live tables and gauges
detail
    _Ultimatum_ draws a terminal user interface the way a layout engine draws a window: a
    tree of **panes** is solved against the terminal's size into rectangles, each pane
    paints into its own rectangle, and an event loop — the **form** — re-solves and
    repaints as the user types, the terminal resizes, or a background task changes what
    a pane shows. It does not hand out a cursor and a screen; it hands each pane an
    `Extent` confined to its rectangle, which is both a `Board` to draw on and a `Stdio`,
    so a bare `Out.println` in a pane body lands inside that pane and nowhere else.

    A layout is built with a small DSL: `panel` for content, `editor` and `menu` for input
    fields, `gauge` for a progress display, `tabular` for a live table, `scrolling` for a
    viewport, and `stack`, `strip` and `grid` to split space between them by weight and
    bounds (`Tui.Sizing`). `conduct` runs the result interactively, either `Tui.Occupancy.Fullscreen`
    on the alternate screen or `Tui.Occupancy.Inline` as a block at the cursor which keeps the
    scrollback; `layout` prints a static block once; `paint` composites onto any `Board`.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import threads.platformThreads
    import probates.cancelProbate

    supervise:
      interactive: terminal ?=>
        conduct(Tui.Occupancy.Inline):
          stack
            ( panel(minHeight = 1, maxHeight = 1)(Out.print(t"  Name")),
              editor(),
              menu(List(t"Save", t"Discard"), t"Save") )
    ```

    Tab cycles focus between the fields, Escape or Ctrl-C ends the form, and every other
    event goes to the focused field. The layout is _reactive_: a `Fixture` reports the
    size its current content needs on every solve, so an editor which wraps onto a second
    line pushes its siblings down, a table whose data changed re-negotiates its columns,
    and a `Tui.Panes` container appended to while the form runs re-tiles it. A `Reading` cell
    assigned from a background task repaints the gauge which shows it.

    The library has three components, and an application takes only the ones it needs:

    - `ultimatum.core` — the layout model (`Tui.Pane`, `Tui.Panes`, `Tui.Sizing`, `Tui.Arrangement`,
      `Tui.Frame`, `Tui.Placement`, `Tui.Region`), the DSL (`panel`, `editor`, `menu`, `scrolling`,
      `stack`, `strip`, `grid`, `border`), the drivers (`conduct`, `layout`, `paint`,
      `Form`), the surfaces (`Extent`, `FlowExtent`, `InlineRoot`, `ScreenRoot`), the
      fixtures (`Fixture`, `Focus`, `EditorField`, `MenuField`, `ScrollFixture`), the
      animation clock `Tick`, and the inline-rendering policies `InlineAnchoring`,
      `InlineGrowth` and `InlineShrink` with their choice packages.
    - `ultimatum.gauges` — spinners, progress bars, meters, sparklines, counters, timers
      and step indicators, chosen by the _type_ of the status they display (`Fraction`,
      `Reckoning`, `Standing`, `Duration`, `Countdown`, `Transfer`, `Meter`,
      `Sequence[Double]`, `Sequence[Step]`, any of them `Captioned` or `Optional`), held in
      a `Reading` cell and shown with `gauge` in a layout, `whilst` at the cursor for the
      duration of a block, or `gaugeLine`/`gaugeRows` as one frame of text. The design is
      selected by the `Gaugeable` given in scope, the colours by a `GaugePalette` and the
      character repertoire by `Gauging`, each an independent import; it depends on
      `aviation` and `quantitative` for durations and byte quantities, which is why it is
      a separate component.
    - `ultimatum.tables` — `TableFixture` and `tabular`, an escritoire `Tabulation` as a
      live pane which lays its columns out afresh at every width; separate so that
      `escritoire` is not imposed on every user of the core.

    The choice packages are imported by name, since `import ultimatum.*` (or
    `import soundness.*`) does not import givens: `inlineAnchoring`, `inlineGrowth` and
    `inlineShrink` select how an inline block behaves on resize (each has a default in its
    enum's companion, so nothing need be imported); `spinners`, `bars`, `meters`,
    `sparklines`, `counters`, `standings`, `processions`, `timers`, `captions`,
    `gaugeGlyphs`, `informationPrefixes` and `palettes` select a gauge's design, caption
    placement, glyphs, byte prefixes and colours. A gauge also needs a `Text is Measurable`
    (`textMetrics.uniformMetric` or `textMetrics.eastAsianScriptsMetric`) in scope, since a
    design is laid out by the width of what it draws.

    Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella, and the choice packages merge with
    the umbrella's own; `border` is the one DSL constructor the umbrella does not currently
    forward, so it is reached as `ultimatum.border`. The interactive drivers run inside
    profanity's `interactive` session, and so need a `Monitor` (from `supervise`) and a
    `Probate`; the in-memory surfaces (`ScreenRoot(width, height)`,
    `InlineRoot(width, height)`, `FlowExtent`) need only a `Stdio`, which is how the layout
    is tested without a terminal.

refer ultimatum.conduct
refer ultimatum.Tui.Pane
refer ultimatum.Form
refer ultimatum.Fixture
refer ultimatum.Gaugeable
refer ultimatum.TableFixture

entry .conduct   runs a pane layout interactively, fullscreen or inline, until the user exits
  detail
      `conduct(mode)(pane)` drives a `Form` over the terminal of the enclosing `interactive`
      session: it solves the layout, paints every pane, then loops over the terminal's
      events until Escape or Ctrl-C (or Ctrl-D) ends it. Tab cycles focus between the
      focusable fixtures (`editor`, `menu`, `scrolling`), tracked by identity so focus
      survives a pane inserted before it; any other keypress goes to the focused fixture; a
      `Terminal.Info.WindowSize` re-tiles the layout to the new size; and a
      `Terminal.Info.Redraw` — which a `Tui.Panes` mutation, a `Reading` update, a
      `TableFixture#refresh` or an animation tick puts on the event spool — repaints.

      `Tui.Occupancy.Fullscreen` (the default) takes over the alternate screen buffer for the
      session, restored on exit, fills the terminal's height, hides the cursor except in a
      focused editor, and repaints only the cells which changed, so an unchanged frame
      writes nothing. `Tui.Occupancy.Inline` draws a block at the cursor, sized to the height
      its content needs, leaving the scrollback intact and the cursor on a fresh line below
      the block when the form ends; its resize repaints are throttled to about ten a second
      and debounced by 50 ms of quiet, so a window drag must pause before the block is
      redrawn, while typing stays immediate.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import threads.platformThreads
      import probates.cancelProbate

      supervise:
        interactive: terminal ?=>
          conduct(Tui.Occupancy.Fullscreen):
            stack
              ( panel(minHeight = 1, maxHeight = 1)(Out.print(t"  Compose")),
                strip
                  ( menu(List(t"Inbox", t"Sent", t"Drafts"), t"Inbox", minWidth = 12, maxWidth = 12),
                    editor(LineEditor(mode = LineEditor.Mode.Multiline(_ => false))) ),
                panel(minHeight = 1, maxHeight = 1)(Out.print(t"  [Tab] focus   [Esc] quit")) )
      ```

      It needs a `Terminal` (from `interactive`), a `Monitor` (from `supervise`) and a
      `Probate` for the deferred-repaint task, and an `InlineAnchoring`, `InlineGrowth` and
      `InlineShrink` policy, each defaulted in its companion and overridden by importing one
      given from `inlineAnchoring`, `inlineGrowth` or `inlineShrink`. A layout with an
      animated gauge arms one timer at the shortest period any of its fixtures asks for;
      a layout of static panes arms none and is purely event-driven.

      The entry point is named `conduct` rather than `form` because telekinesis's HTML form
      renderer owns `form` in the `soundness` umbrella; the class it drives keeps the name
      `Form`.
  refer ultimatum.Form
  refer ultimatum.Tui.Occupancy
  refer ultimatum.layout

entry .layout   prints a pane layout once as a static block at the cursor
    `layout(pane)` solves and paints the pane tree into an `InlineRoot` over the session's
    terminal, presents it as a block of the height its content needs, and leaves the cursor
    on a fresh line below it — a one-shot rendering with no event loop, for a report or a
    summary which is drawn and then scrolled past like any other output. It needs a
    `Terminal` and the three inline policies (`InlineAnchoring`, `InlineGrowth`,
    `InlineShrink`), which default from their companions.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import threads.platformThreads
    import probates.cancelProbate

    supervise:
      interactive: terminal ?=>
        layout:
          strip(panel()(Out.print(t"left")), panel()(Out.print(t"right")))
    ```

    A focusable fixture in the layout is painted unfocused, since nothing will ever
    receive input; `conduct` is the interactive counterpart.

entry .paint   solves a pane tree against a `Board` once and paints every leaf into its rectangle
    `paint(root, pane)` is the drawing step on its own, with no terminal session and no
    event loop: the pane is projected to a `Tui.Frame`, solved against the root's width (and,
    for an `InlineRoot`, the height the content measures; for any other board, the board's
    height), and each leaf's content or fixture is rendered into a `FlowExtent` over its
    rectangle, which is then composited onto the root and the root flushed. This is how a
    layout is rendered into an in-memory surface for testing, or onto a `TerminalBoard`
    with no buffering at all.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    given Stdio = Stdio(null, null, null, termcapDefinitions.basicTermcap)
    val root = FlowExtent(TerminalBoard(8, 3), Tui.Region(0, 0, 8, 3))

    paint(root, stack(panel()(Out.print(t"top")), panel()(Out.print(t"bottom"))))
    root.render   // t"top     \nbottom  \n        "
    ```

    A `FlowExtent` used as the root is itself a `Board`, and its `render` reads back the
    composed grid as plain text, one row per line. A `Fixture` painted this way is given
    `focused = false`.

entry .panel   a leaf pane whose content is drawn into its solved rectangle
    `panel(fraction, minWidth, maxWidth, minHeight, maxHeight)(content)` is the basic
    building block: a `Tui.Pane.Leaf` whose `content` runs once the layout has been solved and
    the pane's rectangle is known, with that rectangle's `Extent` in context. The `Extent`
    is a `Board` — `move`, `put`, `clear` — and a `Stdio`, so `Out.print` inside the body
    writes into the panel; text wraps at the panel's width and scrolls when it overflows
    the height, like a small terminal, and never bleeds into a neighbour.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val title: Tui.Pane = panel(minHeight = 1, maxHeight = 1)(Out.print(t"  Activity"))

    val status: Tui.Pane = panel(fraction = 2.0):
      val extent = summon[Extent]
      extent.move(Prim, Prim)
      extent.put(t"─"*extent.width)
    ```

    `fraction` is the panel's weight when its parent splits space (the default `1.0` is an
    equal share); the bounds pin it on either axis, so `minHeight = 1, maxHeight = 1` is a
    single row which never grows and `minWidth = 20, maxWidth = 20` a fixed column. A panel
    cannot take focus; `editor` and `menu` are the input-taking leaves.

entry .editor   a focusable line-editor pane, whose height grows as its text wraps
    `editor(initial, fraction, minWidth, maxWidth, minHeight, maxHeight)` is a
    `Tui.Pane.Widget` hosting an `EditorField` over a profanity `LineEditor`. The field is
    drawn by the shared `Interaction[Text, LineEditor]`, shows the hardware cursor while it
    has focus and hides it otherwise, and reports as its intrinsic height the number of
    rows its value wraps onto at the width it was last given, so an editor which overflows
    one line pushes the panes below it down. Its value is read back from the
    `EditorField`, which is why a form that needs the text afterwards builds the field
    itself and wraps it in `Tui.Pane.Widget`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val field = EditorField()
    val pane: Tui.Pane = Tui.Pane.Widget(Tui.Sizing(minHeight = 1), field)
    field.handle(Keypress.CharKey('h'))
    field.handle(Keypress.CharKey('i'))
    field.value   // t"hi"
    ```

    `LineEditor(mode = LineEditor.Mode.Multiline(_ => false))` gives a multiline box in
    which Enter inserts a newline and Up and Down move between lines.

entry .menu   a focusable selection-menu pane, one row per option
    `menu(options, current, fraction, minWidth, maxWidth, minHeight, maxHeight)` is a
    `Tui.Pane.Widget` hosting a `MenuField` over a profanity `SelectMenu`: `options` are any
    `Showable` values and `current` the one initially selected. Up and Down move the
    selection; the selected option is marked `>` while the menu has focus and `·` when it
    does not, so the selection is always visible and the focused menu is distinguished. The
    hardware cursor stays hidden in a menu. Its intrinsic height is the number of options.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val sidebar: Tui.Pane =
      menu(List(t"Overview", t"Compose", t"Settings"), t"Overview", minWidth = 20, maxWidth = 20)
    ```

entry .scrolling   a viewport pane over a `ScrollFixture`, scrolled with Up and Down when focused
    `scrolling(fixture)` is a `Tui.Pane.Widget` with a default `Tui.Sizing` over a
    `ScrollFixture` the caller has already built: the fixture, not its content, is the
    parameter, so the caller keeps the handle and can call `scroll`, `toBottom` and
    `refresh` on it while the form runs. Whatever rectangle the layout assigns becomes the
    viewport, and a scrollbar appears in its rightmost column when the content is taller.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val log = ScrollFixture()(List(e"first line", e"second line"))
    val pane: Tui.Pane = scrolling(log)
    ```

entry .strip   splits space between panes side by side, distributing width
    `strip(panes*)` places its children left to right on one row, each as tall as the
    strip and as wide as its `Tui.Sizing` earns: the width is shared by `fraction`, a child
    whose minimum exceeds its share is fixed at that minimum and the rest re-shared, and a
    child capped by its maximum likewise. `strip(panes: Tui.Panes)` is the same split over a
    live container, whose children may be appended to or removed while a form runs.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val frame = strip(panel(minWidth = 8)(()), panel()(())).frame
    frame.arrange(Tui.Region(0, 0, 10, 1)).cells.map(_.width)   // List(8, 2)
    ```

    The name is the `Tui.Arrangement.Strip` it builds; `stack` is the vertical counterpart.

entry .stack   splits space between panes one above another, distributing height
    `stack(panes*)` places its children top to bottom, each as wide as the stack and as
    tall as its `Tui.Sizing` earns by the same rule as `strip`: share by `fraction`, fix any
    child at a minimum or maximum it would otherwise cross. `stack(panes: Tui.Panes)` is the
    same split over a live container. A bounded single-row panel (`minHeight = 1,
    maxHeight = 1`) at the top and bottom of a stack is how a title bar and a status bar
    are pinned while the middle takes the rest.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val frame = stack(panel()(()), panel()(())).frame
    frame.arrange(Tui.Region(0, 0, 8, 4)).cells   // List(Tui.Region(0, 0, 8, 2), Tui.Region(0, 2, 8, 2))
    ```

    The name is the `Tui.Arrangement.Stack` it builds.

entry .grid   flows panes row-major into aligned columns, with an optional gap
    `grid(columns, gap)(panes*)` lays its children out in `columns` columns, filling each
    row left to right before starting the next. Column widths are negotiated across every
    row — a column is as wide as its widest member needs, so cells align as in a table —
    and each row is as tall as its tallest member's minimum, so the grid's height is rigid
    at the sum of its rows. `gap` leaves that many blank cells between columns and between
    rows. An incomplete final row keeps its columns' widths.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val cells = grid(2, gap = 1)
      ( panel(minHeight = 2)(()), panel(minHeight = 2)(()),
        panel(minHeight = 2)(()), panel(minHeight = 2)(()) )

    cells.frame.arrange(Tui.Region(0, 0, 11, 10)).cells
    // List(Tui.Region(0, 0, 5, 2), Tui.Region(6, 0, 5, 2), Tui.Region(0, 3, 5, 2), Tui.Region(6, 3, 5, 2))
    ```

    The name is the `Tui.Arrangement.Grid` it builds.

entry .border   wraps a pane in a box-drawing border, on whichever sides are asked for
    `border(style, top, right, bottom, left)(child)` surrounds `child` with rules drawn in
    a `BorderStyle` (`BorderStyle.light` by default): each requested side is a one-cell
    panel whose rule is regenerated from its solved size, so an edge always spans the
    bordered region exactly and re-rules itself when the layout resizes, and a corner is
    drawn only where two requested sides meet. A partial border is therefore a plain rule:
    `border(top = false, left = false, right = false)` underlines a heading with a single
    line and no corners. The border reserves one row or column on each side it draws,
    which the solver adds to the child's minimum size.

    ```scala fuel
    alternative Full
      code
          val boxed: Tui.Pane = ultimatum.border()(panel()(Out.print(t"hi")))
          // ┌──┐
          // │hi│
          // └──┘
    alternative Rounded
      code
          val boxed: Tui.Pane = ultimatum.border(BorderStyle.rounded)(panel()(Out.print(t"hi")))
          // ╭──╮
          // │hi│
          // ╰──╯
    alternative Sides only
      code
          val boxed: Tui.Pane = ultimatum.border(top = false, bottom = false)(panel()(Out.print(t"hi")))
          // │hi│
    alternative Rule beneath
      code
          val boxed: Tui.Pane =
            ultimatum.border(top = false, left = false, right = false)(panel()(Out.print(t"hi")))
          // hi
          // ──
    ##
    ```

    `border` is not forwarded by the `soundness` umbrella, so it is written as
    `ultimatum.border` (or imported with `import ultimatum.border`).

entry .dirtyCells   which leaves must be repainted: those which moved, resized or changed
    `dirtyCells(previous, current, changed)` compares the leaf rectangles of the last
    solve with those of this one, by position, and returns the indices whose rectangle
    differs (an index beyond the previous sequence is new, and so dirty), together with the
    indices in `changed`, whose content is known to have changed this frame. A leaf whose
    rectangle and content are both unchanged is omitted and left untouched on screen — the
    basis of the fullscreen form's flicker-free redraw.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val before = Sequence(Tui.Region(0, 0, 10, 1), Tui.Region(0, 1, 10, 1))
    val after  = Sequence(Tui.Region(0, 0, 10, 2), Tui.Region(0, 2, 10, 1))
    dirtyCells(before, after, Set())    // Set(0, 1)
    dirtyCells(before, before, Set(1))  // Set(1)
    ```

entry .Tui   the namespace for ultimatum's terminal-interface vocabulary, starting with its layout model
  detail
      Every type a layout is described, solved and placed with lives here: the content-bearing
      `Pane` tree which `panel`, `strip`, `stack` and `grid` build, the `Panes` container a
      running form re-tiles, the pure `Frame` a pane projects to for solving, and the
      `Placement` of `Region`s which solving produces. They are nested because each name,
      bare, would claim a far more general meaning than ultimatum gives it.

  entry .Pane   the content-bearing layout tree: a leaf with content, a widget, or a split
    detail
        A `Pane` is what the DSL builds and the drivers consume. It is an enum of three
        shapes. `Leaf(sizing, content)` is a panel (`panel`): a `Sizing` and a function from
        the solved `Extent^` to `Unit`, run once the rectangle is known. `Widget(sizing,
        fixture)` hosts a live `Fixture` (`editor`, `menu`, `scrolling`, `gauge`, `tabular`)
        which keeps its own state, reports its intrinsic size and may take focus.
        `Branch(sizing, arrangement, panes)` splits its space among the children of a `Panes`
        container along an `Arrangement` (`stack`, `strip`, `grid`).

        A pane tree is pure: a leaf's content is a pure function of its `Extent`, which is
        itself the `Board` and `Stdio` it draws into, so panes can be collected and traversed
        freely. The tree is projected to a `Frame` for solving with `#frame` and walked for
        its leaves with `#leaves`, in the same order, so a solved `Placement`'s cells pair
        one-to-one with the leaves.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val tree: Tui.Pane = stack(panel()(Out.print(t"a")), strip(panel()(()), editor()))
        tree.leaves.size                                   // 3
        tree.frame.arrange(Tui.Region(0, 0, 10, 4)).cells.size   // 3
        tree.weight(2.0).sizing.fraction                   // 2.0
        ```
    refer ultimatum.Tui.Panes
    refer ultimatum.Tui.Sizing
    refer ultimatum.Tui.Frame
    refer ultimatum.Fixture
    entry #sizing   how this pane is sized within its parent: its weight and bounds
    entry #frame   the pure layout structure, with content discarded, for the solver
        A `Leaf` or a `Widget` becomes a `Frame.Cell` of its declared `Sizing` alone; a
        `Branch` becomes a `Frame.Split` over its container's _current_ contents, so a
        mutated `Panes` re-solves. A running `Form` does not use this projection directly: it
        raises each widget's minimum to the size its content measures at the width it last
        occupied, which is how growing content pushes the rest of the layout around.
    entry #leaves   the leaf panes in frame order: left to right in a strip, top to bottom in a stack
        A `Leaf` or `Widget` is its own single leaf; a `Branch` yields its children's leaves
        concatenated. The order matches the cells of the `Placement` which `#frame` solves to.
    entry #weight   a copy of this pane re-weighted with a new `fraction`, for use as a child of a split
        The content, fixture or children are shared with the original; only the `Sizing`'s
        fraction changes.

  entry .Panes   a mutable, ordered container of child panes which a running form re-tiles to
    detail
        `Panes(initial*)` holds the children of a `stack(panes)` or `strip(panes)` split.
        Holding the reference lets the layout change while a form runs: `#append`, `#prepend`,
        `#insert`, `#insertBefore`, `#insertAfter` and `#remove` alter the container, the
        form picks the change up the next time it re-derives its tree, and once the
        container is bound into a running form a mutation also wakes the event loop, so a
        pane added from a background task appears immediately. Each mutation is one atomic
        compare-and-set transition, so concurrent mutations from several tasks lose nothing.
        Panes are compared by identity, so the same pane value is what `insertBefore` and
        `remove` look for.

        ```scala fuel
        frame
          label A log with one line
          code
              val lines = Tui.Panes(panel(minHeight = 1, maxHeight = 1)(Out.print(t"started")))
              val log: Tui.Pane = stack(lines)
              lines.size   // 1
        frame
          label A line appended while the form runs
          code
              val lines = Tui.Panes(panel(minHeight = 1, maxHeight = 1)(Out.print(t"started")))
              val log: Tui.Pane = stack(lines)
              lines.append(panel(minHeight = 1, maxHeight = 1)(Out.print(t"compiling")))
              lines.size   // 2; a running form re-tiles to show the new row
        frame
          label A line inserted before another
          code
              val lines = Tui.Panes(panel(minHeight = 1, maxHeight = 1)(Out.print(t"started")))
              val log: Tui.Pane = stack(lines)
              val compiling = panel(minHeight = 1, maxHeight = 1)(Out.print(t"compiling"))
              lines.append(compiling)
              lines.insertBefore(compiling, panel(minHeight = 1, maxHeight = 1)(Out.print(t"resolving")))
              lines.size   // 3: started, resolving, compiling
        ##
        ```

        The container is not a capability: panes are pure, and its one effectful field, the
        repaint callback, is installed by the form which runs it.
    refer ultimatum.Tui.Pane
    refer ultimatum.stack
    refer ultimatum.strip
    entry #contents   the current children, in order, as a `Sequence`
    entry #size   the number of children
    entry #apply   the child at a zero-based index
    entry #append   adds a pane at the end
    entry #prepend   adds a pane at the start
    entry #insert   inserts a pane at a position, clamped to the container's bounds
    entry #insertBefore   inserts a pane immediately before a reference pane, by identity; appends if absent
    entry #insertAfter   inserts a pane immediately after a reference pane, by identity; appends if absent
    entry #remove   removes a pane, by identity, if present

  entry .Sizing   how a pane is sized within its parent: a fractional weight and per-axis bounds
    detail
        `Sizing(fraction, minWidth, maxWidth, minHeight, maxHeight)` is carried by every pane
        and read by the solver. `fraction` is the pane's relative weight on its parent's
        split axis — siblings weighted 2, 3 and 4 claim 2/9, 3/9 and 4/9 of it, rounded by
        largest remainder so the sizes sum exactly to the space — and the bounds are
        content-driven limits on each axis: a child whose fair share would fall outside its
        bound on the split axis is fixed at that bound and removed from the fractional pool,
        and the rest re-share what remains. An `Unset` maximum is unbounded. Every DSL
        constructor takes these five as named parameters, so a `Sizing` is written out only
        for a hand-built `Pane.Widget`.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val frame = strip(panel(fraction = 2.0)(()), panel(fraction = 3.0)(()), panel(fraction = 4.0)(())).frame
        frame.arrange(Tui.Region(0, 0, 100, 1)).cells.map(_.width)   // List(22, 33, 45)
        ```
    entry #fraction   the relative weight on the parent's split axis; `1.0` by default
    entry #minWidth   the least width the pane may be given; `0` by default
    entry #maxWidth   the most width the pane may be given, or `Unset` for no limit
    entry #minHeight   the least height the pane may be given; `0` by default
    entry #maxHeight   the most height the pane may be given, or `Unset` for no limit

  entry .Arrangement   how a split arranges its children: stacked, in a strip, or in a grid
      The value a `Pane.Branch` or `Frame.Split` carries, named for the constructor which
      builds it so the value and the call read the same. `Stack` puts the children one above
      another, distributing height; `Strip` places them side by side, distributing width;
      `Grid(columns, gap)` flows them row-major into `columns` columns whose widths are
      negotiated across every row, with `gap` blank cells between columns and rows. A
      `Frame#measure` takes an `Arrangement` to say which axis to measure: `Strip` for width,
      anything else for height.

  entry .Occupancy   how much of the terminal an interactive layout occupies: inline or fullscreen
      The mode `conduct` and `Form` run in. `Fullscreen` takes over the terminal through the
      alternate screen buffer (restored on exit), fills its fixed height, and repaints only
      the cells which changed. `Inline` draws a variable-height block at the cursor, sized
      to its content, without the alternate buffer, so the terminal's scrollback stays intact
      and the block remains on screen after the form ends, with the cursor on a fresh line
      beneath it. The enum was once named `Mode`; it was renamed so as not to clash with
      aperture's `Mode` in the `soundness` umbrella.

  entry .Frame   the pure layout tree the solver works on: cells and splits with sizings
    detail
        A `Frame` is a `Pane` with its content discarded: `Cell(sizing)` for a leaf or
        widget, `Split(sizing, arrangement, children)` for a branch. Solving against a root
        `Region` is two passes. `#measure` is bottom-up: each node's minimum and maximum on an
        axis, where a split's minimum along its own axis is forced up to the sum of its
        children's (a container can be no smaller than its contents), its maximum down to the
        sum of theirs (any unbounded child makes the sum unbounded), and across the other
        axis the minimum is the largest child minimum and the maximum the smallest child
        maximum. `#arrange` is top-down: each child becomes a flex track — its measured
        minimum, its fraction as weight, its measured maximum as cap — and the space is
        distributed by fraction, any child which hits a bound fixed there, then recursed.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val frame = Tui.Frame.Split
          ( Tui.Sizing(), Tui.Arrangement.Strip,
            List(Tui.Frame.Cell(Tui.Sizing(1.0, minWidth = 8)), Tui.Frame.Cell(Tui.Sizing(1.0))) )

        frame.measure(Tui.Arrangement.Strip)                       // Tui.Limits(8, Unset)
        frame.arrange(Tui.Region(0, 0, 10, 1)).cells.map(_.width)   // List(8, 2)
        ```

        A grid is solved with the same track solver across its columns — each column's fold
        of its members' limits, weighted by its members' largest fraction — and its rows are
        content-sized at the tallest minimum in each, clamped to the grid's rectangle.
    refer ultimatum.Tui.Pane
    refer ultimatum.Tui.Placement
    refer ultimatum.Tui.Sizing
    entry #sizing   the node's own `Sizing`
    entry #measure   the resolved minimum and maximum of this frame along one axis, as `Limits`
        `measure(Arrangement.Strip)` measures width; any other arrangement measures height.
        A cell's limits are its own sizing's; a split's aggregate its children's as the axis
        and the split's arrangement dictate.
    entry #arrange   solves this frame against a rectangle, producing a `Placement` tree
        A cell takes the whole rectangle. A split divides the rectangle's width (`Strip`) or
        height (anything else) among its children by fraction and bounds, each child getting
        the full extent on the cross axis, and recurses; a grid places each child in its
        negotiated column and content-sized row.

  entry .Placement   the result of solving a layout: a tree of rectangles mirroring the frame tree
    detail
        `Cell(rect)` is a solved leaf and `Split(rect, children)` a solved split; `#cells`
        lists the leaf rectangles in frame order, which is the order of `Pane#leaves`, so the
        two are zipped to pair every leaf with its rectangle.
    entry #region   the rectangle this node was given
    entry #cells   the leaf rectangles in frame order: left to right in a strip, top to bottom in a stack

  entry .Limits   the resolved minimum and maximum size of a frame along one axis
    detail
        Returned by `Frame#measure`. `min` is a cell count and `max` an `Optional[Int]`
        which is `Unset` when the frame is unbounded on that axis.
    entry #min   the least size the frame can be given
    entry #max   the most size the frame can take, or `Unset` for unbounded

  entry .Region   a rectangular region within a surface, in zero-based top-origin coordinates
    detail
        `Region(left, top, width, height)`: `left` and `top` are offsets from the enclosing
        surface's top-left corner and `width` and `height` are cell counts. These are the
        solver's own numbers, so they are plain `Int`s rather than `Ordinal`s; the `Board`
        methods which position a cursor take ordinals (`Prim`, `Sec`, `n.z`).

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val region = Tui.Region(2, 1, 3, 1)
        rect.right            // 5
        rect.bottom           // 2
        rect.contains(4, 1)   // true
        rect.contains(5, 1)   // false
        ```
    entry #left   the zero-based column of the left edge
    entry #top   the zero-based row of the top edge
    entry #width   the width in cells
    entry #height   the height in cells
    entry #right   the column just past the right edge: `left + width`
    entry #bottom   the row just past the bottom edge: `top + height`
    entry #contains   whether a zero-based column and row fall inside the rectangle

entry .BorderStyle   the six glyphs a `border` draws with: two rules and four corners
  detail
      A `BorderStyle` names a horizontal rule, a vertical rule and the top-left, top-right,
      bottom-left and bottom-right corners, each as `Text`. An edge fills its rectangle by
      repeating its rule, so one style serves any size, and a corner is drawn only where
      two requested sides meet. Five styles are provided as companion values, and `.apply`
      builds one from tessellate's box-drawing table so that any line weight has coherent
      corners.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      BorderStyle.light.topLeft     // t"┌"
      BorderStyle.rounded.topLeft   // t"╭"
      BorderStyle.heavy.horizontal  // t"━"
      BorderStyle.double.vertical   // t"║"
      BorderStyle.ascii.topLeft     // t"+"
      ```
  refer ultimatum.border
  entry .apply   a style looked up from a tessellate `LineCharset` and `BoxLine` weight
      `BorderStyle(charset, line)` reads every glyph from the charset by the weights of the
      edges meeting at it — a horizontal rule is `line` on the left and right, a top-left
      corner `line` on the right and bottom, and so on — so the corners always match the
      rules. `BorderStyle(LineCharset.Default, BoxLine.Thin)` is `.light`.
  entry .light   light single lines with square corners; the default
  entry .rounded   light single lines with rounded corners
  entry .heavy   heavy single lines with square corners
  entry .double   double lines
  entry .ascii   `-`, `|` and `+`, for a terminal without box-drawing glyphs
  entry #horizontal   the glyph a horizontal rule repeats
  entry #vertical   the glyph a vertical rule repeats
  entry #topLeft   the top-left corner
  entry #topRight   the top-right corner
  entry #bottomLeft   the bottom-left corner
  entry #bottomRight   the bottom-right corner

entry .Extent   a virtual terminal confined to a rectangle: the `Board` and `Stdio` a panel draws into
  detail
      An `Extent` is what a `panel` body receives in context. It is a `Board`, so a widget
      positions with `move` and writes with `put`, and it is a `Stdio`, so a bare
      `Out.println` in the body flows into the rectangle; because it is a `Stdio`, the
      extent in a panel body shadows any terminal-wide `Stdio` in scope, which is what keeps
      a panel's output inside the panel rather than on the whole screen. `#region` is the
      rectangle it covers within its parent surface. `FlowExtent` is the standard
      implementation.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val ruler: Tui.Pane = panel(minHeight = 1, maxHeight = 1):
        val extent = summon[Extent]
        extent.move(Prim, Prim)
        extent.put(t"·"*extent.width)
      ```
  refer ultimatum.FlowExtent
  refer ultimatum.panel
  entry #region   the rectangle this extent covers within its parent surface

entry .FlowExtent   a `Board` confined to a rectangle of a parent surface, backed by a character grid
  detail
      `FlowExtent(parent, rect)` is the `Extent` the drivers hand to every leaf: writes
      flow and wrap within the rectangle (counting display columns, so a wide CJK glyph
      takes two cells and wraps whole rather than straddling the edge), a newline moves to
      the start of the next row, the grid scrolls up once the last row fills, and
      `#flush` composites the grid onto the parent one row at a time through the parent's
      own `move` and `put`, so positioning is always expressed through the `Board`
      interface and never as inline escapes. The parent is not flushed by the extent: the
      driver presents the root once after every panel has composited, so a frame is
      emitted whole.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      given Stdio = Stdio(null, null, null, termcapDefinitions.basicTermcap)
      val flow = FlowExtent(TerminalBoard(3, 2), Tui.Region(0, 0, 3, 2))
      flow.put(t"abcdefghi")
      flow.render   // t"def\nghi": the grid scrolled

      given (Stdio^{flow}) = flow
      Out.print(t"hi")
      ```

      The grid-backed members it shares with the roots — `width`, `height`, `move`, `put`
      (for `Text` or `Teletype`, so colour survives), `clear`, `clearLine`, `invalidate`
      and `render`, which reads the grid back as plain text with rows joined by newlines —
      are inherited from the common surface. A `FlowExtent` over a `TerminalBoard` is the
      root the tests paint into and read back with `render`.
  refer ultimatum.Extent
  refer ultimatum.paint
  entry #region   the rectangle this extent covers within its parent
  entry #cursor   shows or hides the hardware cursor, through the parent
  entry #showCaret   leaves the caret at an extent-local cell, translated to the parent's coordinates
  entry #flush   composites the grid onto the parent, one row at a time, at the rectangle's offset
  entry #termcap   the `Termcap` the extent reports as a `Stdio`: ANSI, true colour, the rectangle's width
  entry #out   the standard output stream of the extent as a `Stdio`: muted, since rendering goes through `flush`
  entry #err   the standard error stream of the extent as a `Stdio`: muted
  entry #in   the standard input stream of the extent as a `Stdio`: muted
  entry #print   the `Stdio` write: lays text out through the same cursor model as `put`

entry .InlineRoot   the root board for inline mode: a block presented at the cursor by absolute addressing
  detail
      The root `conduct(Tui.Occupancy.Inline)` and `layout` draw into. Panels composite into
      its character grid and `#flush` presents the grid as one write, each row at an
      absolute screen row, so a redraw always lands in the same place and never drifts —
      the property a relative, cursor-tracking present loses the moment a resize reflows
      the lines already on screen. A geometry-stable re-present diffs against a snapshot
      of what the last present drew and overprints only the cells which changed; an
      identical frame emits nothing.

      How the block behaves is governed by three policies read from context when the root
      is built. `InlineAnchoring` says where the block lives: under the default
      `TopAfterResize` it is bottom-docked on launch, appearing beneath the shell command,
      and on the first resize it tries to recover its position from the terminal's reply
      to a cursor query, falling back to pinning itself to the top rows and clearing the
      screen. `InlineGrowth` says what a taller frame does while bottom-docked (scroll the
      screen into scrollback, or grow upward in place); `InlineShrink` what a shorter one
      does (re-dock at the bottom, or hold the top row). Each is defaulted in its
      companion and overridden by one import from `inlineAnchoring`, `inlineGrowth` or
      `inlineShrink`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import stdios.javaLangSystemStdio

      val root = InlineRoot(3, 4)
      root.reframe(3, 2)
      root.move(Prim, Prim)
      root.put(t"ab\ncd")
      root.flush()
      root.finish()
      ```

      `InlineRoot(terminal)` reads the terminal's columns and rows live, so a resize is
      reflected on the next frame, and retains the terminal; `InlineRoot(width, height)`
      is a fixed-size root over the `Stdio` in scope, for testing. An oversize block is
      clamped to the terminal height. The grid members `width`, `height`, `move`, `put`,
      `clear`, `clearLine`, `invalidate` (which marks the next present as a full resize
      repaint; the form driver calls it on every `WindowSize` event) and `render` are
      shared with the other surfaces.
  refer ultimatum.InlineAnchoring
  refer ultimatum.InlineGrowth
  refer ultimatum.InlineShrink
  refer ultimatum.ScreenRoot
  entry .apply   a root over a terminal, read live, or a fixed-size root over a `Stdio`
      `InlineRoot(terminal)` takes the three inline policies from context and the
      terminal's `Stdio`, `knownColumns` and `knownRows`, re-read every frame; its result
      captures the terminal. `InlineRoot(width, height)` takes a `Stdio` and the policies
      from context and is a fresh root of fixed size.
  entry #anchor   stashes the terminal's reply to the resize cursor query, for the next resized present
      `anchor(row, column)` records where the terminal left the parked cursor after
      reflowing the screen on a resize (one-based screen coordinates). The next present
      after `invalidate` uses it to compute where the old block's residue now begins —
      modelling both a reflowing terminal, which re-wraps each line, and a truncating one,
      which clips in place, and trusting whichever model predicts the reply's column —
      clears from there and re-docks, so the scrollback above the block survives. A reply
      matching neither model, or no reply, falls back to the policy's full clear. The
      anchor is consumed by that present, used or not. `Form` does this plumbing itself.
  entry #width   the live width, from the terminal, re-read every frame
  entry #reframe   resizes the grid to the measured block height, clamped to the terminal's rows
      `reframe(width, height)` reallocates and blanks the grid before each frame is
      composited; the driver calls it with the height the content measures.
  entry #cursor   records whether the hardware cursor should be visible, applied by `flush`
  entry #reset   forgets everything recorded about what is on screen, so the next frame is a first frame
      After the caller has cleared the screen itself, this makes the next present render
      at the current cursor as if nothing had been drawn, rather than relative to or
      docked against a prior block.
  entry #showCaret   records the caret's block-local cell; `flush` places the hardware cursor there
  entry #flush   presents the grid as one write: a diff where the geometry is unchanged, else a full redraw
      Under `InlineAnchoring.Flow` the block is drawn relative to the cursor; under every
      other anchoring it is addressed absolutely. The full redraw docks, grows, shrinks and
      clears as the policies say, trims each row to its content (the terminal's erase has
      cleared the rest) so a resize's reflow is predictable, resets the style after any row
      which emitted colour so nothing bleeds, and parks the cursor at the caret cell —
      hidden or not — so a later resize can ask where the block went.
  entry #finish   drops the cursor onto a fresh line below the block and re-shows it
      Called when the form ends, so the following output continues after the rendered
      block, like a submitted prompt. Under `InlineAnchoring.Fullscreen` it also leaves
      the alternate screen buffer, restoring what was there before.

entry .ScreenRoot   the root board for fullscreen mode: a buffered grid presented by diffing against the screen
  detail
      The root `conduct(Tui.Occupancy.Fullscreen)` draws into, inside the alternate screen
      buffer. Panels composite into its character grid and `#flush` presents by diffing
      the grid against a snapshot of what the last present drew, overprinting only the
      damaged runs: an unchanged cell is a no-op and an identical frame emits nothing at
      all. Every present is one write, so partial frames never flash and the resize
      size-probe cannot interleave mid-frame. The first present, and any present after
      `invalidate` (a resize, when the terminal has reflowed whatever was on screen),
      redraws every row at its absolute position.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import stdios.javaLangSystemStdio

      val root = ScreenRoot(3, 2)
      root.move(Prim, Prim)
      root.put(t"ab")
      root.flush()                // draws both rows
      root.move(Sec, Prim)
      root.put(t"X")
      root.flush()                // overprints one cell
      root.render                 // t"aX \n   "
      ```

      `ScreenRoot(terminal)` reads the terminal's size live and retains the terminal;
      `ScreenRoot(width, height)` is a fixed-size root over the `Stdio` in scope. The grid
      members `move`, `put`, `clear`, `clearLine`, `invalidate` and `render` are shared
      with the other surfaces.
  refer ultimatum.InlineRoot
  refer ultimatum.conduct
  entry .apply   a root over a terminal, read live, or a fixed-size root over a `Stdio`
      `ScreenRoot(terminal)` takes the terminal's `Stdio`, `knownColumns` and `knownRows`
      and captures the terminal; `ScreenRoot(width, height)` takes a `Stdio` and is a fresh
      root of fixed size.
  entry #width   the live terminal width, for the solver
  entry #height   the live terminal height, for the solver
  entry #reframe   re-fits the grid to the live terminal size, blanking it and invalidating the snapshot if it changed
      Called by the driver before each solve, so the grid and the terminal only ever
      disagree mid-resize.
  entry #cursor   records whether the hardware cursor should be visible, applied by `flush`
  entry #showCaret   records the caret's cell; `flush` places the hardware cursor there
  entry #flush   presents the grid as one write: only the changed runs, or every row after `invalidate`
  entry #finish   re-shows the hardware cursor on exit; the alternate-screen wrapper restores the screen

entry .InlineAnchoring   where an inline block lives on screen, and what it does when the terminal resizes
  detail
      Read from context by `InlineRoot`, `conduct` and `layout`. `TopAfterResize`, the
      default (`.default`, in the companion), starts bottom-docked — the block appears
      inline beneath the shell command — and on the first resize recovers the block's
      position from the terminal's cursor reply if it can, otherwise switches, once and
      for good, to being pinned to the top rows and clears the screen, because a
      bottom-docked block leaves a gap and strands a ghost when the terminal grows taller,
      whereas a top-anchored one is simply overdrawn in place. `BottomDocked` never
      switches: it clears a bottom band and re-docks on every resize. `TopAnchored` is
      pinned to rows 1 to the block's height from the first frame. `Fullscreen` takes over
      the alternate screen buffer, top-anchored, and restores the previous screen on
      `finish`. `Flow` renders relative to the cursor, flowing with prior output, never
      docking to an edge: growing scrolls the screen naturally and shrinking clears the
      freed rows below.

      ```scala fuel
      alternative Default
        code
            locally:
              given Stdio = Stdio(null, null, null, termcapDefinitions.basicTermcap)
              val root = InlineRoot(3, 4)
              root.reframe(3, 2); root.move(Prim, Prim); root.put(t"ab\ncd"); root.flush()
              // rows 3–4 of the 4-row terminal
      alternative Top-anchored
        code
            locally:
              import inlineAnchoring.topAnchoring
              given Stdio = Stdio(null, null, null, termcapDefinitions.basicTermcap)
              val root = InlineRoot(3, 4)
              root.reframe(3, 2); root.move(Prim, Prim); root.put(t"ab\ncd"); root.flush()
              // rows 1–2
      alternative Bottom-docked
        code
            locally:
              import inlineAnchoring.bottomDockedAnchoring
              given Stdio = Stdio(null, null, null, termcapDefinitions.basicTermcap)
              val root = InlineRoot(3, 4)
              root.reframe(3, 2); root.move(Prim, Prim); root.put(t"ab\ncd"); root.flush()
              // rows 3–4, and still rows 3–4 after a resize
      alternative Fullscreen
        code
            locally:
              import inlineAnchoring.fullscreenAnchoring
              given Stdio = Stdio(null, null, null, termcapDefinitions.basicTermcap)
              val root = InlineRoot(3, 4)
              root.reframe(3, 2); root.move(Prim, Prim); root.put(t"ab\ncd"); root.flush()
              root.finish()   // leaves the alternate screen, restoring what was there
      ##
      ```
  refer ultimatum.inlineAnchoring
  refer ultimatum.InlineRoot
  entry .default   the given in implicit scope when none is imported: `TopAfterResize`

entry .InlineGrowth   what a bottom-docked inline block does when a frame is taller than the last
  detail
      `ScrollIntoScrollback`, the default (`.default`), scrolls the screen up by the extra
      rows, pushing what was above into the scrollback, so the block stays docked at the
      bottom and nothing above it is overwritten. `ClampToScreen` grows upward in place,
      overwriting the rows above without touching the scrollback. Imported by name from
      `inlineGrowth`; read by `InlineRoot` when it is built.
  refer ultimatum.inlineGrowth
  entry .default   the given in implicit scope when none is imported: `ScrollIntoScrollback`

entry .InlineShrink   what an inline block does when a frame is shorter than the last
  detail
      `RedockBottom`, the default (`.default`), moves the block down to stay docked at the
      bottom and clears the rows it vacated above. `KeepTop` holds the top row where the
      taller block began and clears below it, so the block stays put and the freed space
      becomes blank screen beneath it, with `finish` leaving the cursor right under the
      block rather than after the vacated rows. Imported by name from `inlineShrink`; read
      by `InlineRoot` when it is built.
  refer ultimatum.inlineShrink
  entry .default   the given in implicit scope when none is imported: `RedockBottom`

entry .inlineAnchoring   the five anchoring policies for an inline block, each a given to import by name
  detail
      `InlineAnchoring.default` (`TopAfterResize`) applies when nothing is imported; one of
      these replaces it for the `InlineRoot`, `conduct(Tui.Occupancy.Inline)` or `layout` call
      in scope. `import soundness.*` does not bring them in.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import inlineAnchoring.topAnchoring
      ```
  entry .bottomDockedAnchoring   `InlineAnchoring.BottomDocked`: always docked at the bottom, re-docking on every resize
  entry .topAnchoring   `InlineAnchoring.TopAnchored`: pinned to the top rows from the first frame
  entry .topAfterResizeAnchoring   `InlineAnchoring.TopAfterResize`: bottom-docked, then top-anchored after the first resize
  entry .fullscreenAnchoring   `InlineAnchoring.Fullscreen`: the alternate screen buffer, top-anchored
  entry .flowAnchoring   `InlineAnchoring.Flow`: drawn relative to the cursor, flowing with prior output

entry .inlineGrowth   the two growth policies for a bottom-docked inline block, each a given to import by name
  detail
      `InlineGrowth.default` (`ScrollIntoScrollback`) applies when nothing is imported.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import inlineGrowth.clampedGrowth
      ```
  entry .scrollbackGrowth   `InlineGrowth.ScrollIntoScrollback`: scroll the screen up, pushing rows into scrollback
  entry .clampedGrowth   `InlineGrowth.ClampToScreen`: grow upward in place, overwriting the rows above

entry .inlineShrink   the two shrink policies for an inline block, each a given to import by name
  detail
      `InlineShrink.default` (`RedockBottom`) applies when nothing is imported.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import inlineShrink.keepTopShrink
      ```
  entry .redockBottomShrink   `InlineShrink.RedockBottom`: re-dock at the bottom, clearing the rows vacated above
  entry .keepTopShrink   `InlineShrink.KeepTop`: hold the top row and clear below it

entry .Fixture   a live element the layout can host: it keeps state, renders itself and reports its size
  detail
      A `Fixture` is what a `Tui.Pane.Widget` carries. It owns its own mutable state, renders
      itself onto whichever `Board` the driver gives it — so the driver can move it to a
      new rectangle on re-layout — and reports through `#measure` the intrinsic size its
      current content needs, which is the hook the reactive layout uses to grow or shrink
      a pane: a running `Form` raises each widget's minimum width and height to what it
      measures at the width it last occupied. `#period` is the animation contract: a
      fixture which wants redrawing on a clock says how often, the form arms one timer at
      the shortest period over the live fixtures, and arms none when every fixture is
      static.

      A fixture need not accept input: a gauge does not, and so stays out of the Tab cycle.
      `Focus` adds input handling on top, and `EditorField`, `MenuField` and
      `ScrollFixture` are the focusable fixtures in the core; `TableFixture` and the gauge
      fixture are display-only.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      class Clock extends Fixture:
        def render(canvas: Board^, focused: Boolean): Unit =
          canvas.move(Prim, Prim)
          canvas.put(t"tick")

        def measure(width: Int): (Int, Int) = (4, 1)
        override def period: Optional[Int] = 1000

      val pane: Tui.Pane = Tui.Pane.Widget(Tui.Sizing(minHeight = 1, maxHeight = 1), Clock())
      ```
  refer ultimatum.Focus
  refer ultimatum.Tui.Pane
  refer ultimatum.Form
  entry #render   draws the fixture onto a board, told whether it currently has focus
      `render(canvas, focused)`: the canvas is the `FlowExtent` over the fixture's solved
      rectangle, so drawing is relative to its top-left corner and clipped to its size.
      A `paint`-driven host passes `focused = false`.
  entry #measure   the minimum width and height the fixture's current content needs at a given width
      `measure(width)` returns `(minWidth, minHeight)`; the form takes the larger of each
      and the pane's declared `Tui.Sizing` bound. A fixture which claims no intrinsic size
      returns `(0, 1)` and takes whatever rectangle the sizing gives it.
  entry #period   how many milliseconds until the fixture wants redrawing again, or `Unset` if only on change
      A spinner returns its frame interval; a bar, an editor or a table returns `Unset`.
      A fixture with a period is repainted on every frame whether or not anything else
      about it changed, since its appearance depends on the clock.

entry .Focus   a fixture which accepts input: the focusable kind, cycled with Tab
  detail
      `Focus` extends `Fixture` with `#handle`, which folds one terminal event into the
      fixture's state. A `Form` keeps the focusable fixtures in leaf order, moves focus
      between them on Tab (tracked by identity, so it survives an inserted pane), sends
      every keypress other than Tab, Escape and Ctrl-C to the focused one, and repaints
      both the pane losing focus and the pane gaining it. `EditorField`, `MenuField` and
      `ScrollFixture` implement it.
  refer ultimatum.Fixture
  refer ultimatum.EditorField
  refer ultimatum.MenuField
  entry #handle   folds a terminal event into the fixture's state

entry .EditorField   a focusable fixture wrapping a `LineEditor`, as tall as its wrapped text
  detail
      The fixture `editor` builds. It holds a profanity `LineEditor`, draws it through the
      shared `Interaction[Text, LineEditor]` which also positions the caret, shows the
      hardware cursor only while focused, and reports as its height the rows its value
      wraps onto at the width it was last given, so an editor that grows past one line
      pushes the rest of the layout down. `#value` is the current text.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val field = EditorField(LineEditor(t"ab\ncd", mode = LineEditor.Mode.Multiline(_ => false)))
      field.handle(Keypress.Up)
      field.handle(Keypress.CharKey('X'))
      field.value                             // t"abX\ncd"
      EditorField(LineEditor(t"aaaaa")).measure(3)   // (0, 2)
      ```
  refer ultimatum.editor
  entry #value   the editor's current text
  entry #render   draws the editor and its caret; the hardware cursor is shown only when focused
  entry #handle   applies a keypress or other event to the line editor
  entry #measure   `(0, rows)`: no minimum width, and one row per wrapped line of the value

entry .MenuField   a focusable fixture wrapping a `SelectMenu`, one row per option
  detail
      The fixture `menu` builds, over a profanity `SelectMenu` of `Showable` items. Up and
      Down move the selection; the selected option is marked ` > ` while the menu has focus
      and ` · ` otherwise, every other option is indented three cells, and the hardware
      cursor is kept hidden since a menu has no caret. `#value` is the selected item.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val field = MenuField(SelectMenu(List(t"a", t"b", t"c"), t"a"))
      field.handle(Keypress.Down)
      field.value   // t"b"
      ```
  refer ultimatum.menu
  entry #value   the currently selected item
  entry #render   draws the options with the selection marked, pointer when focused and dot when not
  entry #handle   applies a keypress to the select menu
  entry #measure   `(0, options)`: no minimum width, and one row per option

entry .ScrollFixture   a viewport over content taller than its space, with a scrollbar and a position
  detail
      `ScrollFixture(track, thumb)(content)` shows the window of `content` — a by-name
      list of `Teletype` lines, re-read on every render, so a growing log is seen as it
      grows — that its scroll position selects, and draws a scrollbar in its rightmost
      column whenever the content is taller than the viewport: a track of `track` glyphs
      (the light vertical rule by default) with a `thumb` (the heavy one) whose height is
      proportional to the visible fraction and whose position to the scroll offset. When
      the content fits, no bar is drawn and the full width is used; content is clipped to
      the remaining width so a long line never wraps into the bar.

      The position is either _docked to the bottom_, the initial state, in which the
      viewport follows the end of the content as it grows — a live log follows its tail —
      or an explicit offset, which holds still while content is appended. Scrolling down
      to the bottom re-docks. Under a form the Up and Down keys scroll when the fixture is
      focused; a `paint`-driven host calls `#scroll` directly.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val lines = List(e"one", e"two", e"three", e"four")
      val viewport = ScrollFixture()(lines)
      val pane: Tui.Pane = scrolling(viewport)

      viewport.atBottom     // true
      viewport.scroll(-1)   // up one line, holding still as content is appended
      viewport.toBottom()   // docked again
      ```

      The fixture holds the content supplier for its lifetime, so the supplier must stay
      valid while the hosting form runs.
  refer ultimatum.scrolling
  entry .apply   a viewport over by-name content, with the scrollbar's track and thumb glyphs
      `ScrollFixture(track = BorderStyle.light.vertical, thumb = BorderStyle.heavy.vertical)(content)`.
  entry #refresh   wakes the running form to re-render, after the content has changed
  entry #atBottom   whether the viewport is docked to the bottom, tracking growth
  entry #toBottom   docks the viewport to the bottom again and wakes the form
  entry #scroll   moves the viewport by a number of lines, negative for up; reaching the bottom re-docks
      Scrolling up from the docked state first materialises the current bottom offset;
      the request is clamped to the content length and viewport height last rendered.
  entry #handle   `Keypress.Up` scrolls up one line and `Keypress.Down` down one; other events are ignored
  entry #measure   `(0, 1)`: no intrinsic size; whatever rectangle the sizing gives becomes the viewport
  entry #render   draws the visible window of lines and, when they overflow, the scrollbar in the last column

entry .Form   the interactive driver: re-solves, repaints and routes events over a pane tree
  detail
      A `Form` is what `conduct` builds and runs; it is used directly to drive a layout
      over a root of one's own choosing — an in-memory `FlowExtent` or `ScreenRoot` for a
      test, with a hand-made event iterator. `Form(root, mode, pane, wake, throttle,
      debounce, scheduleWake)` takes the `Board` to draw on, the `Occupancy`, the pane
      tree, a `wake` callback which puts a `Terminal.Info.Redraw` on the event spool (bound
      into every `Tui.Panes` and `Fixture` so a background change repaints), the resize
      `throttle` and `debounce` in milliseconds, and `scheduleWake`, which arranges a
      deferred wake after a delay — the debounced resize repaint and the animation tick
      both use it. `#run` then loops over an event iterator until it is exhausted or the
      user exits.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      given Stdio = Stdio(null, null, null, termcapDefinitions.basicTermcap)
      val root = FlowExtent(TerminalBoard(10, 4), Tui.Region(0, 0, 10, 4))

      val events = List
        ( Keypress.CharKey('h'), Keypress.CharKey('i'),
          Keypress.Tab,
          Keypress.CharKey('y'), Keypress.CharKey('o'),
          Keypress.Escape )

      Form(root, Tui.Occupancy.Fullscreen, stack(editor(), editor())).run(events.iterator)
      root.render   // t"hi        \n          \nyo        \n          "
      ```

      The pane tree is re-derived from its live containers on every frame, so panes
      appended or inserted while the form runs are picked up and the layout re-tiles;
      each widget's minimum is raised to the size its content measures. In fullscreen a
      frame repaints only the leaves `dirtyCells` names (plus every animated fixture),
      clearing the screen first when the number of leaves changed or the geometry is
      stale; inline re-measures the block's height and re-presents it whole. A
      `WindowSize` event marks the geometry stale and, with a debounce, coalesces until
      the drag pauses, during which a keypress updates its widget but does not present;
      the inline root is also handed the cursor reply so it can recover the block's
      position. A form is a stateful, exclusive capability: it is owned by the one call
      that runs it, held in a local or passed as `Form^`, and a closure which captures it
      cannot call `run`.
  refer ultimatum.conduct
  refer ultimatum.Tui.Pane
  refer ultimatum.Tui.Occupancy
  refer ultimatum.dirtyCells
  entry .apply   a fresh form over a board, mode and pane tree, with optional wake and timing parameters
      `Form(root, mode, pane, wake = () => (), throttle = 0, debounce = 0, scheduleWake = _ => ())`
      returns a `Form^` which retains its root and its wake callbacks. `conduct` passes
      the terminal's spool as `wake`, a `snooze`-then-redraw task as `scheduleWake`, and
      `100`/`50` as the inline throttle and debounce.
  entry #run   drives the form over an iterator of terminal events until it is exhausted or the user exits
      `run(events)` paints the first frame, then for each event: Tab moves focus to the
      next focusable fixture and repaints both; Escape, Ctrl-C or Ctrl-D ends the loop;
      `Interrupt.Winch` and `Terminal.Info.WindowSize` start a coalesced resize repaint;
      `Terminal.Info.CursorPosition` is stashed as the resize anchor;
      `Terminal.Info.Redraw` repaints (or reschedules its wake while a resize is still
      pending); any other `Interrupt` is ignored; and any other event goes to the focused
      fixture's `handle`, whose pane is then repainted. An `InlineRoot` is `finish`ed when
      the loop ends. The events are a murmuration `Iterator`, so a hand-rolled source is
      `new Iterator[Terminal.Event]` with `advance()` and `current`, or `list.iterator`.

entry .Tick   the animation clock reading handed to a gauge design each time it is drawn
  detail
      `Tick(index, elapsed)`: `index` counts whole animation periods since the gauge
      started — the frame number a spinner indexes its glyph cycle with — and `elapsed` is
      monotonic milliseconds since it started. Passing the reading in, rather than letting
      a design read a clock, keeps every design a pure function of its status and its
      tick, so a test drives one by handing it a literal `Tick` instead of waiting.
      `gaugeLine` and `gaugeRows` take a tick (`Tick.zero` by default), and a `Gaugeable`'s
      `rows(status, tick, width)` is where it is consumed.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import gaugeGlyphs.unicodeGlyphs
      import palettes.emberGaugePalette
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric

      Tick.at(240, 80)             // Tick(3, 240)
      Tick.at(240, 80).phase(80)   // 0.0
      Tick.at(100, 80).phase(80)   // 0.25

      gaugeLine(Fraction(0.0), 1, Tick.at(2*80, 80))(using spinners.brailleDotsSpinner).plain   // t"⠹"
      ```
  refer ultimatum.Gaugeable
  refer ultimatum.gaugeLine
  entry .zero   the reading at the start: index 0, nothing elapsed
  entry .at   the reading for an elapsed time under a period: `Tick(elapsed/period, elapsed)`
      `Tick.at(elapsed, period)`; a period of zero or less gives index 0.
  entry #index   whole animation periods since the gauge started: the frame number
  entry #elapsed   monotonic milliseconds since the gauge started
  entry #phase   where the reading falls within the current period, as a fraction in `[0, 1)`
      `phase(period)` is `(elapsed % period)/period`, the dial a design uses when it wants
      a continuous sweep rather than a discrete frame; `0.0` for a period of zero or less.

entry .TableFixture   a live escritoire table as a pane, laid out afresh at every width
  detail
      `TableFixture(content)` hosts a by-name `Tabulation[Teletype]` — any `Scaffold`'s
      `tabulate(rows)`, or a list's `tabulation` — re-read on every measure, so changed
      data is what the next frame shows. Each `#measure` lays the table out at the width
      the layout offers and the columns renegotiate as escritoire's engine decides:
      wrapping a paragraph column, shrinking, or collapsing a column below its threshold,
      exactly as a static `grid(width)` would; `#render` replays the lines that measure
      settled on. A fixture cannot raise, so a width no column arrangement can satisfy
      renders overflowing (escritoire's `ignoreAttenuation`) rather than failing. Call
      `#refresh` after changing the underlying data to wake the form and re-render.

      It needs a `Table.Style`, a `Text is Measurable` and a `Hyphenation` in scope when
      built, which are the same three a static table needs. `tabular` wraps one in a pane.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import tableStyles.thinRoundedTableStyle
      import hyphenations.englishHyphenation
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric

      case class Entry(name: Text, total: Int)
      val entries = List(Entry(t"alpha", 1), Entry(t"beta", 22))

      val fixture = TableFixture:
        Scaffold[Entry, Teletype]
          ( Column[Entry, Text, Teletype](e"Name")(_.name),
            Column[Entry, Int, Teletype](e"Count")(_.total) )
        . tabulate(entries)

      fixture.measure(40)   // (17, 6): the rendered width and height
      ```

      The fixture holds the content supplier for its lifetime, so the supplier must stay
      valid while the hosting form runs. It takes no input, so Tab skips over it.
  refer ultimatum.tabular
  refer ultimatum.Fixture
  entry .apply   a fixture over by-name table content, with the style, metric and hyphenation in scope
      `TableFixture(content: => Tabulation[Teletype])(using Table.Style, Text is Measurable,
      Hyphenation)`.
  entry #refresh   wakes the running form to re-measure and re-render, after the data has changed
  entry #measure   lays the table out at a width and returns its rendered width and row count
      `(widest line, lines)`: the widest rendered row by display width, and the number of
      rows including rules and titles.
  entry #render   draws the lines the last measure produced, re-laying out if the canvas width differs

entry .tabular   a pane hosting a live table, from by-name `Tabulation[Teletype]` content
    `tabular(content)` is a `Tui.Pane.Widget` with a default `Tui.Sizing` over a `TableFixture`:
    the table is laid out afresh at whatever width the layout gives it, on every frame,
    and its data is re-read each time. It needs a `Table.Style`, a `Text is Measurable` and
    a `Hyphenation` in scope. The style is fixed when the pane is built, so a table under
    a different style is a different pane:

    ```scala fuel
    alternative Thick
      code
          locally:
            import tableStyles.thickTableStyle
            import hyphenations.englishHyphenation
            import textMetrics.uniformMetric
            case class Entry(name: Text, total: Int)
            val pane: Tui.Pane = tabular(List(Entry(t"alpha", 1), Entry(t"beta", 22)).tabulation[Teletype])
            // ┏━━━━━━━┯━━━━━━━┓
            // ┃ Name  │ Total ┃
            // ┠───────┼───────┨
            // ┃ alpha │     1 ┃
            // ┃ beta  │    22 ┃
            // ┗━━━━━━━┷━━━━━━━┛
    alternative Thin rounded
      code
          locally:
            import tableStyles.thinRoundedTableStyle
            import hyphenations.englishHyphenation
            import textMetrics.uniformMetric
            case class Entry(name: Text, total: Int)
            val pane: Tui.Pane = tabular(List(Entry(t"alpha", 1), Entry(t"beta", 22)).tabulation[Teletype])
            // ╭───────┬───────╮
            // │ Name  │ Total │
            // ├───────┼───────┤
            // │ alpha │     1 │
            // │ beta  │    22 │
            // ╰───────┴───────╯
    alternative Minimal
      code
          locally:
            import tableStyles.minimalTableStyle
            import hyphenations.englishHyphenation
            import textMetrics.uniformMetric
            case class Entry(name: Text, total: Int)
            val pane: Tui.Pane = tabular(List(Entry(t"alpha", 1), Entry(t"beta", 22)).tabulation[Teletype])
            //   Name    Total
            // ╶───────────────╴
            //   alpha       1
            //   beta       22
    ##
    ```

    In a running form the table is a display-only fixture: it never takes focus, and a
    change to the data is shown after `TableFixture#refresh`, or on the next repaint for
    any other reason. To call `refresh`, build the `TableFixture` yourself and wrap it in
    `Tui.Pane.Widget`.

entry .Gaugeable   a design for drawing a status type as a terminal gauge: a bar, a spinner, a meter, a checklist
  detail
      `Gaugeable` is the typeclass behind every gauge. An instance for a status type says how
      a value of that type is drawn at a given width and animation `Tick`, how narrow it can
      usefully go (`#minWidth`), how wide it would like to be (`#columns`), how many rows it
      takes (`#height`), whether it stretches to fill what it is offered (`#elastic`), and
      how often it wants redrawing regardless of its status (`#period`). Rendering is pure: a
      status, a tick and a width map to styled `Teletype` rows, so a design captures nothing,
      can sit inside a pane tree, and can be tested by calling `#rows` with a literal `Tick`.

      The status *type* is the key. `Fraction`, `Reckoning`, `Standing`, `Duration`,
      `Countdown` and `Captioned` have default designs in their companions; `Transfer`,
      `Meter`, `Sequence[Double]` and `Sequence[Step]` deliberately have none, because their
      candidate designs differ in height or in what the numbers mean. Any other design is
      chosen by importing one given by name from a choice package — `bars`, `spinners`,
      `meters`, `sparklines`, `counters`, `standings`, `processions` or `timers` — and the
      application code is unchanged.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric
      import palettes.emberGaugePalette

      gaugeLine(Fraction(0.5), 10).plain                            // t"█████     "

      locally:
        import bars.arrowheadBar
        gaugeLine(Fraction(0.5), 10).plain                          // t"[===>    ]"
      ```

      Every design given takes a `Gauging` — the palette, glyph repertoire and text metric in
      scope where the given is summoned — so the appearance is decided by the user's imports
      at the line which builds the gauge, not by the code which later draws it. Each design
      renders *exactly* the width it is given, at every width, degrading as it narrows (a bar
      becomes a percentage at four cells and a single shade at one) rather than overrunning.

      Any status may be `Optional`: `.optional` lifts the design for `T` to one for
      `Optional[T]`, drawing `#absent` when the value is `Unset`, so a job whose total is not
      yet known uses the same type as one whose total is.
  refer ultimatum.Gauging
  refer ultimatum.GaugePalette
  refer ultimatum.Reading
  refer ultimatum.gauge
  refer ultimatum.gaugeLine
  entry .optional   lifts the design for a status to the same status made `Optional`
      A given which derives `Optional[T] is Gaugeable` from any `T is Gaugeable` in scope.
      The `Mandatable` constraint identifies the mandatory type, so the lift applies only to
      genuine optionals and never competes with the definite instance — which keeps
      `Captioned(Fraction(0.5), …)` unambiguous when both are in scope. The lifted design
      draws the definite one's `#rows` when the value is present and its `#absent` rows
      when it is `Unset`; it reports a `#period` of the inner design's or 80 ms, since an
      absent value may have to sweep even where the definite design never animates.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric
      import palettes.emberGaugePalette

      gaugeLine(Fraction.indeterminate, 10).plain                   // t"██░░░░░░░░", sweeping
      gaugeLine(Fraction.indeterminate, 10, Tick.at(240, 80)).plain // t"░░░██░░░░░"
      ```
  entry .Glyphs   how adventurous a design may be with its characters: `Ascii`, `Unicode`, `Braille` or `Emoji`
    detail
        A repertoire, held in scope as a `Gaugeable.Glyphs` given and consulted through
        `Gauging#permits`. The repertoires nest: `Unicode` permits the BMP box-drawing and
        block glyphs, `Braille` additionally the braille patterns, and `Emoji` additionally
        the two-cell emoji; `Ascii` permits seven-bit output only. The companion's `.default`
        is `Unicode`, which every modern terminal has; a different repertoire is imported by
        name from `gaugeGlyphs`, and degrades or promotes the whole catalogue at once, since
        every design carries an ASCII rendering and every exotic design names a narrower
        fallback.

        `Emoji` is distinct from `Unicode` because emoji are the only glyphs which occupy two
        cells, and a terminal which renders them at one cell shears every row that uses them.
    entry .default   the no-import repertoire: `Unicode`, box-drawing and block glyphs with no emoji
        A companion given, so it is in implicit scope with no import; any
        `import gaugeGlyphs.…` outranks it.
  entry .Fixture   binds a pure design to a live `Reading` and the form's repaint machinery
    detail
        The adapter through which a gauge becomes a pane the layout can host, built by
        `gauge` and rarely named directly: `Gaugeable.Fixture(reading)` summons the status's
        design where it is called, so the palette, glyphs and metric it draws with are the
        caller's. It keeps a monotonic clock of its own, so a spinner does not jump when the
        system clock is stepped and two spinners started at different moments stay
        independently phased. A fixture is not a `Focus`, so Tab skips over it.
    entry #measure   the design's preferred columns and its row count at a width
        The width reported is the design's `columns` for the current status, which the solver
        treats as a minimum; an elastic design is given more if the layout has it.
    entry #render   paints the current status's rows into the canvas it is given
        Draws nothing when the canvas is narrower than the design's `minWidth`, rather than
        something broken; rows beyond the canvas's height are clipped.
    entry #period   the design's animation period, so the form can arm one timer for all its gauges
  entry .Row   a base class for a design occupying exactly one row
    detail
        Nearly every design is one row. `Row[status]` fixes `Self` to the status and asks
        only for `#row`, wrapping it as a one-element list for `rows`.
    entry #row   the single row at a width and tick
    entry #rows   `row`, as a one-element list
  entry #period   how often, in milliseconds, the design wants redrawing regardless of its status
      `Unset` for a design which changes only when its status does — a bar, a counter, a
      timer. A spinner returns its frame interval; a checklist 80 ms for its running step's
      marker. A conducted layout takes the shortest period over the gauges on screen and
      runs one timer for all of them, and none when nothing animates.
  entry #minWidth   the narrowest width at which the design renders something meaningful
      Given less, the driver draws nothing rather than something broken. One cell for a bar
      (a single shade), three for a checklist, the narrowest fallback's width for a spinner.
  entry #columns   the width the design would like
      A bar's preferred width (`Bar.defaultColumns`, 40 cells); exactly the width a spinner,
      a status glyph or a counter occupies. Defaults to `minWidth`.
  entry #height   the rows the design occupies at a width
      One for a bar or a spinner, one per step for a checklist, five for a thermometer. This
      is what lets a gauge push the rest of the layout around.
  entry #elastic   whether the design takes all the width it is offered, or is held at `columns`
      `true` for a bar; `false` for a spinner, a status glyph, a counter or a timer, which
      `gauge` pins to their own width so they do not stretch across the terminal.
  entry #rows   renders the status at exactly a width, for a tick
      Every row measures exactly `width` cells under the ambient metric, and there are
      exactly `height(status, width)` of them. The tick is the animation clock reading; a
      test drives a design with `Tick.zero` or `Tick.at(elapsed, period)`.
  entry #absent   how to draw when there is no status at all, the `Unset` of an `Optional`
      "Not measured" is a different claim from "no progress", so a bar sweeps here rather
      than sitting empty, and a spinner draws its frames. The default is a blank row, right
      for a design with nothing to say without a value. `.optional` calls it.
  entry #absentColumns   the width to claim when there is no status to measure
      One cell by default; a bar claims its usual width so the sweep has room to travel.

entry .Gauging   the rendering context a design captures: its palette, glyph repertoire and text metric
  detail
      Every design given takes a `Gauging`, assembled by `.assembled` from three separately
      importable givens — a `GaugePalette`, a `Gaugeable.Glyphs` and a `Text is Measurable` —
      so a caller overrides any one axis (the colours, the characters, the width metric)
      without naming the other two. The design captures it where its given is summoned, so
      the user's imports at the line which builds the gauge decide its appearance.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric
      import palettes.solarizedDarkGaugePalette
      import gaugeGlyphs.asciiGlyphs

      val context: Gauging = summon[Gauging]
      context.permits(Gaugeable.Glyphs.Unicode)   // false
      context.cells(t"compiling")                 // 9
      ```

      A design never writes an escape sequence directly: every colour goes through `#tint`
      or `#wash`, and every width through `#cells`, which is what makes one design correct
      under any palette, repertoire and metric.
  refer ultimatum.GaugePalette
  refer ultimatum.Gaugeable
  entry .assembled   the given which builds a `Gauging` from the palette, glyphs and metric in scope
      In implicit scope with no import. It needs a `GaugePalette` (the companion's `adaptive`
      given, from the `Termcap` in scope, unless one is imported from `palettes`), a
      `Gaugeable.Glyphs` (`Unicode` by default) and a `Text is Measurable` (imported by name,
      such as `textMetrics.uniformMetric` or `textMetrics.eastAsianScripts`).
  entry #palette   the colours to draw in
  entry #glyphs   the repertoire the designs may use
  entry #metric   how a text's width in cells is measured
  entry #tint   colours a fragment's foreground from the palette
      `tint(color)(text)` wraps the `Teletype` in `Fg(color)`. Threading every colour through
      here means a design writes no escape itself.
  entry #wash   colours a fragment's background
      `wash(color)(text)` wraps the `Teletype` in `Bg(color)`; a bar whose track is drawn as
      spaces shows the track as a background this way.
  entry #permits   whether a required repertoire is available under the one in scope
      The repertoires nest: `Ascii` is always permitted, `Unicode` whenever the scope is not
      `Ascii`, `Braille` under `Braille` or `Emoji`, and `Emoji` only under `Emoji`. A design
      asks this to pick its best available rendering, or to follow its fallback chain.
  entry #cells   the display width of a text in cells, under the metric in scope

entry .GaugePalette   the colours a gauge draws with, named by role rather than by hue
  detail
      A `GaugePalette` names its colours by the part each plays — `#track`, `#fill`,
      `#leadingEdge`, `#caption`, `#muted`, `#success`, `#warning` and `#danger`, with
      `background` and `foreground` from `Palette` — so one design renders under any palette
      and one palette serves every design. Ten ship in the `palettes` package, imported by
      name; with no import, `.adaptive` picks one from what the terminal reports.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric
      import palettes.oceanicGaugePalette

      gaugeLine(Fraction(0.7), 20)
      ```

      Two ramps derive from the roles: `#lengthwise`, the fill interpolated along a bar's
      length, and `#severity`, the fill as a function of a reading's value, which is what a
      battery or a load meter uses. A palette of your own implements the eight role colours
      (and the two from `Palette`) and may override either ramp.

      It is a real trait rather than a structural refinement of `Palette`, since structural
      member selection goes through runtime reflection, which Scala Native does not have.
  refer ultimatum.palettes
  refer ultimatum.Gradient
  entry .adaptive   the no-import palette, chosen from the terminal's colour depth
      A companion given which needs a `Termcap` in scope: a terminal reporting no colour or
      eight indexed colours gets `palettes.monochromeGaugePalette`, sixteen colours or the
      4-level cube `palettes.ansiSixteenGaugePalette`, and the 6-level cube or true colour
      `palettes.slateGaugePalette`. Any `import palettes.…` outranks it, because a
      lexically-scoped given beats a companion one; a caller who names a palette has
      asserted they know what their terminal does.
  entry #Form   the colour form every role is given in: `Srgb`
  entry #track   the unfilled part of a bar, the hollow pips, the rail a needle travels
  entry #fill   the filled part of a bar, and a spinner's glyph
  entry #leadingEdge   the tip of an arrowhead bar, the head of a marker, and the running step
      Brighter than `#fill`, so a bar reads as moving even in a still screenshot. A smooth
      bar's partial boundary cell is drawn in `#fill` (or `#lengthwise` at its position on a
      gradient design), so it matches the full cells to its left; only a `Bar.Glyphs#tip`
      takes this colour.
  entry #caption   labels, captions and figures
  entry #muted   present but not competing: completed steps, elapsed times, units, a rate
  entry #success   a succeeded step, and the calm end of the severity ramp
  entry #warning   a warned step, and the middle of the severity ramp
  entry #danger   a failed step, and the far end of the severity ramp
  entry #lengthwise   the fill interpolated along a bar's length, as a `Gradient`
      Position 0 is the left edge and 1 the right. Only a gradient design (`bars.gradientBar`,
      a sparkline's cells, a one-cell shade) consults it; the default is the two-stop ramp
      from `#fill` to `#leadingEdge`.
  entry #severity   the fill as a function of a reading's value, from `success` through `warning` to `danger`
      Held at `#success` across the lower half, then climbing through `#warning` to `#danger`:
      `Gradient(success, success, warning, danger)`. A battery reads it backwards, since a
      battery reddens as it empties, and an urgent countdown reads it over its last minute.
  entry #steps   one background per step of a ribbon, spread along the lengthwise ramp
      `steps(count)` samples `#lengthwise` at `count` evenly spaced positions, for
      `processions.ribbonProcession`.
  entry #colorOf   the colour a `Standing` is drawn in
      `Succeeded` is `#success`, `Failed` `#danger`, `Warned` `#warning`, `Skipped` `#muted`,
      `Pending` `#track` and `Running` `#leadingEdge`.

entry .Gradient   an N-stop colour ramp over sRGB, sampled at a position in `[0, 1]`
  detail
      `Gradient(stop1, stop2, …)` builds a ramp from evenly spaced stops, and `gradient(0.3)`
      interpolates between the two stops either side of that position, channel by channel in
      sRGB. A repeated stop flattens that part of the ramp, which is how `GaugePalette#severity`
      holds one colour across the whole lower half before it starts to climb. A one-stop
      gradient returns its stop everywhere, and an empty one black.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val ramp = Gradient(Srgb(0.0, 0.0, 0.0), Srgb(1.0, 1.0, 1.0))
      ramp(0.5)   // Srgb(0.5, 0.5, 0.5)
      ```
  refer ultimatum.GaugePalette
  entry .apply   a gradient from its stops, as a variadic argument list
  entry #stops   the stops, in order from position 0 to position 1
  entry #apply   the colour at a position, clamped to `[0, 1]`

entry .Fraction   a proportion in `[0, 1]`, clamped: the status a progress bar or a spinner draws
  detail
      `Fraction` is the opaque type `ultimatum.internal.Fraction`, exported here: a `Double`
      clamped to `[0, 1]` (`NaN` reads as zero), distinct from `Double` so that a progress
      bar's design can be keyed on it without claiming every `Double` in the program.
      `Fraction(0.42)` makes one, `Fraction.of(done, total)` divides, and the extensions
      `value`, `percentage` and `complete` read it back.

      Its companion supplies the default design, `bars.smoothBar`, so a `Fraction` draws as
      a smooth eighth-block bar with no import; any other bar or any spinner is imported by
      name from `bars` or `spinners`. A spinner keys on `Fraction` too and ignores the value,
      so importing one in place of a bar is how indeterminate work is shown — and two designs
      for `Fraction` in one scope conflict, so a layout wanting both binds one as a local
      given.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric
      import palettes.emberGaugePalette

      gaugeLine(Fraction(0.42), 3).plain                    // t"42%"
      gaugeLine(Fraction.of(3, 4), 8).plain                 // t"██████  "
      Fraction(1.2).complete                                // true: clamped to 1.0
      ```

      Progress which may not be known is `Optional[Fraction]`, with `Fraction.indeterminate`
      as the unknown; `Gaugeable.optional` lifts any `Fraction` design to it, and a bar
      sweeps when the value is `Unset`, so a job which starts out unmeasurable and later
      learns its total never changes type.
  refer ultimatum.bars
  refer ultimatum.spinners
  refer ultimatum.internal

entry .Countdown   time left, clamped at zero: the status a countdown timer draws
  detail
      `Countdown` is the opaque type `ultimatum.internal.Countdown`, exported here: an
      `aviation.Duration` clamped at zero, so a deadline which has passed reads as `0s`
      rather than as a negative interval. `Countdown(5.0*Minute)` makes one, and the
      extension `duration` reads it back. Elapsed time is a plain `Duration`; a countdown
      keeps its own type only so that the two can sit side by side with different designs,
      and so that a countdown can redden as it runs out (`timers.urgentCountdown`).

      Its companion supplies the default design, `timers.compactCountdown`, which writes the
      two largest units that carry information (`2m41s`); `timers.digitalCountdown` gives a
      clock face instead.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric
      import palettes.emberGaugePalette

      gaugeLine(Countdown(161.0*Second), 5).plain   // t"2m41s"
      gaugeLine(Countdown(-5.0*Second), 2).plain    // t"0s"
      ```
  refer ultimatum.timers
  refer ultimatum.Stopwatch

entry .Reading   a mutable cell holding a gauge's current status; assigning to it repaints the gauge
  detail
      `Reading(initial)` is what `gauge`, `whilst` and `Inlay` take: a cell whose status may
      be updated from any task. `reading()` reads it, `reading() = status` publishes a new
      value and wakes the running form or inlay so the gauge repaints at once, and `#amend`
      transforms it atomically. The status itself is plain data, so a pane tree holding a
      reading stays pure.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric
      import palettes.emberGaugePalette
      import stdios.javaLangSystemStdio

      val progress = Reading(Fraction(0.0))
      val work = List(1, 2, 3, 4, 5)

      supervise:
        async:
          work.each: item =>
            snooze(0.5*Second)
            progress() = Fraction.of(item, work.size)

        interactive: terminal ?=>
          conduct(Tui.Occupancy.Inline)(stack(gauge(progress)))
      ```

      The status type is fixed when the reading is made, so a job whose total is not yet
      known starts as `Reading(Fraction.indeterminate)`, of type
      `Reading[Optional[Fraction]]`, and assigns a `Fraction` later.
  refer ultimatum.gauge
  refer ultimatum.whilst
  entry #apply   the current status
      Visible across threads: a value assigned on one task is what the next paint, on
      another, draws.
  entry #update   `reading() = status`: publishes a new status and wakes the gauge
      Assignment syntax, paired with `apply`. Until the reading is bound into a running form
      or inlay the wake is a no-op, so a reading may be updated before it is displayed.
  entry #amend   transforms the status atomically, as a compare-and-set
      `reading.amend(_ + 1)` applies the function to the current status and publishes the
      result; two tasks amending at once both land. The function may be re-run under
      contention, so it must be pure.

entry .gauge   a pane hosting a live gauge, drawn by the design in scope for the reading's status type
  detail
      `gauge(reading)` builds a `Tui.Pane.Widget` for a `Reading`, using the `Gaugeable` given
      for its status type at the call site, so which design is used is decided by the
      caller's imports at that line. The gauge reports its design's intrinsic size on every
      solve: an elastic design (a bar) fills what it is given, an inelastic one (a spinner,
      a status glyph, a counter) is pinned to its own width, and a multi-row design (a
      checklist) grows the layout to its step count. A design which animates drives the
      form's frame clock at its `period`. A gauge accepts no input, so Tab skips over it.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric
      import palettes.emberGaugePalette
      import processions.checklistProcession

      val steps: Sequence[Step] =
        Sequence
          ( Step(t"resolve", Standing.Succeeded),
            Step(t"compile", Standing.Running),
            Step(t"publish", Standing.Pending) )

      val checklist: Tui.Pane = gauge(Reading(steps))
      ```

      The optional parameters `fraction`, `minWidth`, `maxWidth`, `minHeight` and `maxHeight`
      are the pane's `Tui.Sizing`, as for any other pane; `minWidth` is raised to the design's
      own minimum, and `maxWidth` is ignored for an inelastic design.
  refer ultimatum.Reading
  refer ultimatum.Gaugeable
  refer ultimatum.whilst

entry .gaugeRows   one frame of a status as styled rows, at a width and tick
    The pure entry point, for a caller doing its own drawing: `gaugeRows(status, width)`
    is the design's `rows` for the status type's `Gaugeable` in scope, at `Tick.zero` unless
    a tick is given. A multi-row design (a checklist, a thermometer, a tall sparkline)
    returns one `Teletype` per row.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import textMetrics.uniformMetric
    import palettes.emberGaugePalette
    import processions.checklistProcession

    val steps: Sequence[Step] =
      Sequence(Step(t"resolve", Standing.Succeeded), Step(t"publish", Standing.Pending))

    gaugeRows(steps, 9).map(_.plain)   // List(t"✓ resolve", t"· publish")
    ```

entry .gaugeLine   one frame of a status as a single line, at a width and tick
    What a bar, a spinner, a counter or a timer renders to. A multi-row design yields only
    its first row here; `gaugeRows` gives them all. A gauge always needs a width — there is
    no rendering of a bar which does not know how wide it is — so a status has no
    `Teletypeable` of its own, and this is the way to print one.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import textMetrics.uniformMetric
    import palettes.emberGaugePalette
    import stdios.javaLangSystemStdio

    Out.println(gaugeLine(Fraction(0.42), 40))
    Out.println(gaugeLine(Reckoning(17, 120), 7))
    Out.println(e"${gaugeLine(Standing.Succeeded, 1)} built")
    ```

    A caller which redraws for itself passes the tick: `gaugeLine(status, width,
    Tick.at(elapsedMillis, period))` picks the spinner frame for that moment.

entry .whilst   shows a gauge at the cursor for the duration of a block, then erases it
  detail
      The standalone path, with no form and no layout: `whilst(reading)(block)` starts an
      `Inlay` over the reading, evaluates the block, and erases the gauge in a `finally`,
      leaving the cursor on a clean line. Every design works here unchanged. The animation
      runs in one background task redrawing in place at the design's period; a design with
      no period is drawn once and then only when the reading changes. It needs a `Stdio`, a
      `Monitor` and a `Probate` in scope, so it is called within `supervise`.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric
      import palettes.emberGaugePalette
      import stdios.javaLangSystemStdio
      import spinners.brailleDotsSpinner

      supervise:
        whilst(Reading(Fraction.indeterminate)):
          snooze(2.0*Second)
      ```

      The optional `width` fixes the gauge's width; otherwise it is the terminal's, from the
      `Stdio`'s termcap.
  refer ultimatum.Inlay
  refer ultimatum.Reading

entry .Inlay   a gauge drawn live at the cursor, with no form and no layout
  detail
      The shape a command-line tool wants when it has one thing to report. `Inlay(reading)`
      (optionally with a fixed `width`) is started with `#start` and ended with `#finish`;
      `whilst` wraps the pair around a block. Each frame is redrawn in place over the last,
      every row erased to the end of the line so a shorter frame leaves no residue, and the
      block is erased on finish. The animation is one background task snoozing the design's
      own period; a design with no period is drawn once and thereafter only when its
      `Reading` changes. It needs a `Stdio`, a `Monitor` and a `Probate`.

      Frames are serialised under a mutex, since the animation task and whichever task
      updates the reading both paint; `#finish` on one thread reliably stops the task and
      suppresses any frame still in flight, so nothing is written after the erase.
  refer ultimatum.whilst
  entry #start   paints the first frame and, if the design animates, starts repainting at its period
  entry #finish   stops the animation, erases the block and leaves the cursor where it started

entry .Bar   how a proportion is drawn: a bar that fills, a travelling marker, a run of pips, or a bare figure
  detail
      `Bar` is the engine behind every design in `bars`, keyed on `Fraction`. Its four cases
      cover the catalogue: `Filled(glyphs, columns, gradient)` is a bar which fills from the
      left using a `Bar.Glyphs` set, `Marker(track, marker, columns)` a head travelling along
      a rail with nothing filled behind it, `Segmented(pip, hollow, gap, pips)` a run of
      discrete pips, and `Numeric` the percentage alone. Each becomes a `Fraction is
      Gaugeable` through `#gaugeable`, which is how the `bars` givens are declared, so a bar
      of your own is one expression:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric
      import palettes.emberGaugePalette

      given hashBar: (Fraction is Gaugeable) =
        Bar.Filled(Bar.Glyphs(t"#", t"", t".", t"<", t">"), 30, false).gaugeable

      gaugeLine(Fraction(0.5), 10).plain   // t"<####....>"
      ```

      Degradation is implemented here once rather than in each design. A bar re-quantizes to
      whatever width it is given; a `Filled` bar drops its end caps below eight cells, where
      they would cost a fifth of the bar; every shape falls through to a right-aligned
      percentage below four cells, and to a single shade glyph (`░▒▓█`, or `.:*#` under
      ASCII) at one. Cell accounting is exact — full cells, at most one boundary cell and
      empty cells always sum to the width — so a bar never changes width as it fills. With
      no `Fraction` to draw, a bar sweeps a lit window back and forth along its track
      (`#sweep`), since an empty bar would claim "no progress" where the truth is "not
      measured".
  refer ultimatum.bars
  refer ultimatum.Fraction
  entry .Glyphs   the characters a filled bar is drawn from: the full cell, its partials, the empty cell, caps and tip
    detail
        `Bar.Glyphs(full, partials, empty, leftCap, rightCap, tip)`. `partials` are the
        intermediate widths of the boundary cell, in ascending order and excluding `full`:
        supplying them is what makes a bar advance by a fraction of a cell rather than a
        whole one (`bars.smoothBar` uses the seven eighth-width blocks `▏▎▍▌▋▊▉`), and an
        empty `partials` gives a bar which steps cell by cell. An `empty` of a space shows
        the track as a background colour; any other glyph is drawn in the track colour. A
        `tip` is a different idea from a partial: it replaces the last filled cell at every
        intermediate value, the classic `[===>    ]`, and is drawn in the palette's
        `leadingEdge` where a partial takes the fill's colour.
    entry #full   the glyph of a filled cell
    entry #partials   the boundary cell's intermediate widths, ascending; empty for a cell-by-cell bar
    entry #empty   the glyph of an unfilled cell; a space shows the track as a background
    entry #leftCap   the glyph before the bar, if any; dropped below eight cells
    entry #rightCap   the glyph after the bar, if any; dropped below eight cells
    entry #tip   a glyph marking where the fill has reached, present at every intermediate value
  entry .defaultColumns   the width every bar prefers: 40 cells
      What a `Filled` or `Marker` bar reports as its `columns`; the layout may give an
      elastic bar more.
  entry #columnCount   the width this bar prefers
      The `columns` of a `Filled` or `Marker`; `pips` plus the gaps between them for a
      `Segmented`; four cells for `Numeric`.
  entry #gaugeable   this bar as a `Fraction is Gaugeable`, under the `Gauging` in scope
      The design is elastic, one row, with a minimum width of one cell and a preferred width
      of `columnCount`; its absent rendering is `#sweep`. Every given in `bars` is one of
      these.
  entry #sweep   the indeterminate rendering: a lit window travelling back and forth along the track
      The window is a fifth of the width, and its position is a triangle wave over the tick
      index, so it returns rather than jumping back to the start. `Gaugeable.optional` draws
      it for an `Optional[Fraction]` which is `Unset`.
  entry #draw   one row of this bar for a fraction, at exactly a width
      The pure rendering, with the degradation to a percentage and to a shade applied:
      `bars.smoothBar` at `Fraction(0.42)` draws `42%` in three cells and `█` in one.

entry .Facet   one horizontal part of a composite gauge row, with an order in which it is given up
  detail
      A composite row — a transfer's figures, rate and estimate; a caption, a bar and a
      percentage — is laid out from `Facet`s. Each has a `shed` priority (the highest value
      is dropped first when the row will not fit), a minimum width, whether it is the one
      flexible part which absorbs the surplus, and a pure `render` from the width it was
      given to its `Teletype`. `.fixed` and `.flexible` make them, and `.solve` lays them
      out.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric
      import palettes.emberGaugePalette

      def row(width: Int): Text =
        Facet.solve
          ( List
              ( Facet.fixed(2, Teletype(t"compiling")),
                Facet.flexible(4)(w => Teletype(t"="*w)),
                Facet.fixed(1, Teletype(t"42%")) ),
            width )
        . plain

      row(24)   // t"compiling ========== 42%"
      row(12)   // t"======== 42%"
      row(4)    // t"===="
      row(2)    // t"  "
      ```

      This is the column-negotiation idea of a table — a declared minimum, a droppable part,
      a flexible remainder — without a search: a gauge's parts have exact widths and never
      reflow, so one greedy pass is exact, and below every minimum the result is spaces.
  entry .fixed   a part whose width is fixed by its own content, with a shed priority
      `Facet.fixed(shed, content)` measures the content under the `Gauging` in scope. The
      highest `shed` is dropped first.
  entry .flexible   the one part which absorbs whatever width is left, and is never shed
      `Facet.flexible(minWidth)(render)`: `render` is handed the minimum plus the surplus. At
      most one facet in a row should be flexible; it is never shed, because a row which has
      dropped its gauge is not worth drawing.
  entry .solve   lays facets out into exactly a width, dropping parts in shed order until the rest fit
      `Facet.solve(facets, width, gap = 1)` joins the kept parts with `gap` spaces. Ties in
      `shed` keep source order, so a row degrades the same way every time rather than
      flickering between two arrangements at a boundary. Total by construction: below every
      minimum the result is `width` spaces.
  entry #shed   how readily this part is given up; the highest value goes first
  entry #minWidth   the cells the part needs
  entry #flexible   whether this part takes the surplus and is never shed
  entry #render   the part's content, as a pure function of the width it actually got

entry .Magnitude   compact renderings of the numbers beside a gauge: durations, counts, percentages, byte sizes
  detail
      Where every cell is contested, a full-precision figure would crowd out the bar. These
      are the writers the counters, timers and transfer designs share: each keeps its field
      narrow and, where it matters, stable in width as the value moves.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Magnitude.interval(161.0)          // t"2m41s"
      Magnitude.count(1200L)             // t"1.2k"
      Magnitude.percentage(Fraction(0.07))   // t"  7%"
      Magnitude.bytes(4200000.0, true)   // t"4.00 MiB"
      Magnitude.rate(1500.0, false)      // t"1.50 kB/s"
      ```
  entry .interval   a duration in seconds as its two largest useful units: `41s`, `2m41s`, `1h04m`, `3d02h`
      Never more than two units and never a leading zero unit, so the field stays narrow
      and stops jittering once past a minute. Negative durations read as `0s`.
  entry .count   a count, abbreviated once it stops being readable at a glance: `947`, `1.2k`, `15k`, `3.4M`
      One decimal place below ten of a unit and none above, so the field is at most four
      cells; the suffixes are `k`, `M` and `G`.
  entry .percentage   a fraction as a percentage three cells wide: `  0%` to `100%`
      Right-aligned, so a bar's figure never shifts as it fills.
  entry .bytes   a byte count in the largest prefix leaving a figure above one: `947 B`, `4.01 MiB`, `1.20 GB`
      `bytes(value, binary)`: `binary` selects the 1024-based prefixes (`KiB`, `MiB`, …) over
      the 1000-based (`kB`, `MB`, …). The two describe the same quantity under different
      conventions, so a design says which it means. Three significant figures below ten,
      otherwise whole units.
  entry .rate   a transfer rate in the same prefixes as `bytes`, with `/s`

entry .CaptionLayout   where a caption sits relative to the gauge it labels, and what gives way when both will not fit
  detail
      `CaptionLayout(gap, trailing, elide)`: the spaces between gauge and label, whether the
      label follows the gauge or precedes it, and whether a long label is shortened rather
      than squeezing the gauge. Eliding is the right priority, since a half-drawn bar
      misreports the thing it is measuring where a shortened label is merely terser: the
      caption is allowed at most half the row before it starts costing the gauge cells. The
      companion's `.default` is one space, trailing, eliding; the `captions` package has the
      alternatives, imported by name. A `Captioned` status takes the layout in scope.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric
      import palettes.emberGaugePalette
      import bars.blockBar

      gaugeLine(Captioned(Fraction(0.5), t"copying"), 12).plain   // t"██░░░ copyi…"

      locally:
        import captions.leadingCaption
        gaugeLine(Captioned(Fraction(0.5), t"go"), 8).plain      // t"go ██░░░"
      ```
  refer ultimatum.captions
  refer ultimatum.Captioned
  entry .default   the no-import layout: the label follows the gauge, one space away, elided when long
      A companion given, in implicit scope; any `import captions.…` outranks it.
  entry #gap   the spaces between the gauge and its label
  entry #trailing   whether the label follows the gauge (`true`) or precedes it
  entry #elide   whether a long label is shortened rather than squeezing the gauge
  entry #gaugeWidth   how many cells the gauge gets beside a caption, within a row width
      The caption's allowance is its measured width, capped at half the row when eliding;
      the gauge gets the remainder less the gap, up to its preferred width.
  entry #compose   one row from a drawn gauge and its caption, padded to the row width
      The caption is shortened with an ellipsis to the room left, tinted in the palette's
      `caption` role, and placed before or after the gauge as `trailing` says.

entry .Captioned   any status with a label beside it: `⠹ resolving dependencies`, `████░░ copying`
  detail
      `Captioned(status, caption)` wraps a status of any type with a `Text` label. Its design
      is derived from whatever design the underlying status has in scope, with the label
      placed by the `CaptionLayout` in scope, so it is structural rather than a style choice
      and needs no import: a captioned `Fraction` draws the bar or spinner a bare `Fraction`
      would, and inherits its animation period.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric
      import palettes.emberGaugePalette
      import spinners.brailleDotsSpinner

      val resolving: Tui.Pane = gauge(Reading(Captioned(Fraction.indeterminate, t"resolving dependencies")))
      gaugeLine(Captioned(Fraction(0.5), t"copying"), 12).plain   // t"⠋ copying   "
      ```

      Only the first row carries the caption; a multi-row design keeps its shape below it.
  refer ultimatum.CaptionLayout
  refer ultimatum.captions
  entry .gaugeable   derives `Captioned[T] is Gaugeable` from the `T is Gaugeable` and `CaptionLayout` in scope
      In implicit scope: the gauge is drawn at the width `CaptionLayout#gaugeWidth` leaves
      it, so a narrow captioned bar both elides its label and degrades its bar
      (`Captioned(Fraction(0.5), t"copying")` at eight cells is `50% cop…`).
  entry #status   the status being labelled
  entry #caption   the label

entry .Checklist   how a run of steps is drawn: `Rows`, `Breadcrumb`, `Beads`, `Numbered` or `Ribbon`
  detail
      The engine behind the `processions` givens, keyed on `Sequence[Step]`. `Rows` is one
      row per step, each marked by its standing with the running step's marker animated
      (`✓ resolve` / `⠋ compile` / `· publish`); the rest fit on one row: `Breadcrumb` joins
      the step names with `›`, `Beads` is a chain of one bead per step joined by a rule
      (`●━◐━○`, fixed at `2n - 1` cells), `Numbered` is the running step's name with a
      `[2/3]` position, and `Ribbon` is a powerline of coloured segments, which needs a
      patched font and so is never a default. Because the designs differ in height,
      `Sequence[Step]` has no default design: the choice decides how much of the layout the
      gauge takes.

      Every rendering has an ASCII form (`+`, `x`, `!`, `-`, `.` and `-\|/` for the marker)
      under `gaugeGlyphs.asciiGlyphs`, and each step's colour comes from
      `GaugePalette#colorOf`.
  refer ultimatum.processions
  refer ultimatum.Step
  refer ultimatum.Standing
  entry #gaugeable   this design as a `Sequence[Step] is Gaugeable`, under the `Gauging` in scope
      `Rows` and `Beads` declare an 80 ms period, since they animate the running step; the
      others have none. The minimum width is three cells; `Rows` is as tall as the sequence
      (at least one row), the rest one row.
  entry #draw   the rows for a run of steps, at a tick and width

entry .Dial   how a bounded reading is drawn: `Battery`, `Thermometer`, `Needle`, `Bullet`, `Column` or `Ascii`
  detail
      The engine behind the `meters` givens, keyed on `Meter`. A meter is not progress — it
      can fall as well as rise — so these designs mark the scale and colour the reading by
      where it sits on it, through `GaugePalette#severity`. `Battery` is capped cells with a
      terminal nub, reddening as it *empties*; `Thermometer` a five-row column read from the
      bottom with a bulb beneath; `Needle` a tick travelling along a marked scale, with
      nothing filled behind it; `Bullet` a measure over a qualitative track whose shaded
      bands say what counts as low, fair and high; `Column` a single cell whose block height
      is the reading; and `Ascii` the `[####----]` form. They differ in height as well as
      width, which is why `Meter` has no default design.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric
      import palettes.emberGaugePalette

      gaugeLine(Meter(0.5), 1)(using meters.columnMeter).plain                // t"▅"
      gaugeLine(Meter(50.0, 0.0, 100.0), 10)(using meters.asciiMeter).plain   // t"[####----]"
      gaugeRows(Meter(1.0), 1)(using meters.thermometerMeter).size            // 5
      ```
  refer ultimatum.meters
  refer ultimatum.Meter
  entry #columnCount   the width the design prefers: 8 for a battery, 12 for a needle, 20 for a bullet, 1 for a column
  entry #rowCount   the rows the design takes: five for a thermometer, otherwise one
  entry #gaugeable   this design as a `Meter is Gaugeable`, under the `Gauging` in scope
      `Column` and `Thermometer` are inelastic, held at one cell; the others stretch.
  entry #draw   the rows for a meter at a width

entry .Meter   a reading on a bounded scale: a battery's charge, a temperature, a load average
  detail
      `Meter(value, minimum, maximum)`, or `Meter(value)` on the unit scale. Distinct from
      `Fraction` because the bounds are part of the datum — a meter shows where a value sits
      between them, and a design may mark the scale — and because a meter is not progress:
      it can fall as well as rise. It has no default design; one of the `meters` givens
      (`batteryMeter`, `thermometerMeter`, `needleMeter`, `bulletMeter`, `columnMeter`,
      `asciiMeter`) is imported by name.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric
      import palettes.emberGaugePalette
      import meters.batteryMeter

      gaugeLine(Meter(0.3), 10)
      Meter(50.0, 0.0, 100.0).fraction.value   // 0.5
      ```
  refer ultimatum.meters
  refer ultimatum.Dial
  entry .apply   `Meter(value)`: a reading on the scale from 0 to 1
  entry #value   the reading
  entry #minimum   the bottom of the scale
  entry #maximum   the top of the scale
  entry #fraction   where the reading sits between the bounds, as a `Fraction`
      Zero when the scale has no span; otherwise `(value - minimum)/(maximum - minimum)`,
      clamped.

entry .Reckoning   a count of work done against a total which may not be known yet: `17/120`, or `17`
  detail
      `Reckoning(done, total = Unset)`: the status a counter shows, and the one most jobs
      actually have to hand. Its companion's default design is `counters.plainCounter`,
      which writes `done/total`, or just `done` while the total is still being discovered;
      the other `counters` givens pad, spell, scale or percentage it.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric
      import palettes.emberGaugePalette

      gaugeLine(Reckoning(17, 120), 7).plain                                  // t"17/120 "
      gaugeLine(Reckoning(17), 2).plain                                       // t"17"
      gaugeLine(Reckoning(1200, 8400), 9)(using counters.scaledCounter).plain // t"1.2k/8.4k"
      ```
  refer ultimatum.counters
  entry .Counter   how a count is written: `Plain`, `Padded`, `Words`, `Percentage` or `Scaled`
    detail
        The engine behind the `counters` givens for `Reckoning`. `Plain` is `17/120`,
        `Words` `17 of 120`, `Percentage` the `Magnitude.percentage` of the fraction (`  ?%`
        with no total), `Scaled` the abbreviated figures `1.2k/8.4k`, and `Padded`
        right-aligns the numerator to the total's digit count (`  7/120`, then `117/120`) so
        the field does not jitter as it counts up — the difference between a figure which
        can be read while it changes and one which cannot.
    entry #write   the count as text, in this style
    entry #gaugeable   this style as a `Reckoning is Gaugeable`, inelastic and one row
        Too narrow for the figure, the design drops its leading characters rather than its
        trailing ones, so the numerator — the part which changes — survives longest.
  entry .gaugeable   the default design, `counters.plainCounter`: `17/120` is not a style question
  entry #done   the work done
  entry #total   the total, or `Unset` while it is not yet known
  entry #fraction   `done/total` as a `Fraction`, or `Unset` while the total is unknown
  entry #remaining   `total - done`, never negative, or `Unset` while the total is unknown

entry .Sparkline   how a run of samples is drawn: `Blocks`, `Tall`, `Dots` or `Ascii`
  detail
      The engine behind the `sparklines` givens, keyed on `Sequence[Double]`. `Blocks` gives
      eight levels in one row (`▁▂▃▄▅▆▇█`), `Tall` stacks two rows for sixteen, and `Dots`
      (`⣀⠤⠒⠉`) and `Ascii` (`_.-^`) trade resolution for a narrower repertoire. Because the
      choice sets the vertical resolution, and so what the reader can see in the data,
      `Sequence[Double]` has no default design.

      Samples are scaled to their own range unless `#scaled` fixes the floor and ceiling,
      and a sparkline narrower than its series is *decimated* by `.decimate` — each cell
      shows the maximum of the samples it covers — never truncated, since a truncated
      sparkline would show only the oldest samples while looking like the whole series.
      Each cell is coloured by its height through `GaugePalette#lengthwise`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric
      import palettes.emberGaugePalette
      import sparklines.blockSparkline

      gaugeLine(Sequence(0.0, 0.5, 1.0), 3).plain                          // t"▁▅█"
      gaugeLine(Sequence(10.0, 20.0), 2).plain                             // t"▁█", auto-scaled
      gaugeLine(Sequence(0.0, 1.0, 0.0, 0.0), 2).plain                     // t"█▁", decimated
      gaugeLine(Sequence(0.0, 5.0), 2)(using Sparkline.Blocks.scaled(0.0, 10.0)).plain   // t"▁▅"
      ```
  refer ultimatum.sparklines
  entry .decimate   reduces a series to exactly a width by taking the maximum of each group
      `Sparkline.decimate(samples, width)` returns the samples unchanged when they already
      fit; otherwise each of the `width` cells covers a contiguous group and keeps its peak,
      so the shape's spikes survive where truncation would lose them.
  entry #rowCount   two rows for `Tall`, otherwise one
  entry #gaugeable   this design as a `Sequence[Double] is Gaugeable`, scaled to the samples it is given
      One cell per sample is the preferred width; elastic, so it may be given more.
  entry #scaled   the same design with the scale fixed to a floor and a ceiling
      `Sparkline.Blocks.scaled(0.0, 100.0)` is a `Sequence[Double] is Gaugeable` whose
      lowest glyph is the floor and highest the ceiling, with either `Unset` to keep that
      end adaptive. A fixed scale is a presentation decision, not part of the data: a
      sparkline which rescales itself every frame makes a steady signal look erratic.
  entry #draw   the rows for a series, with optional bounds, at a width
      A design whose glyphs are not permitted under the `Gaugeable.Glyphs` in scope falls
      back to the ASCII ramp rather than to nothing, and `Tall` collapses to one row there.

entry .Spinner   a cyclic run of frames shown one at a time, with a fallback chain for narrow or plain terminals
  detail
      `Spinner(frames, interval, columns, repertoire, narrower)`: the frames, the
      milliseconds between them, how wide every frame is (they must agree, or the row would
      shear as it animates), the least adventurous `Gaugeable.Glyphs` which can render it,
      and what to fall back to when this design will not fit or is not permitted. The chain
      is what lets an emoji design degrade to a BMP one, and a wide marquee to a single
      spinning cell, without the caller choosing a different design. `.each` builds one from
      single-cell frames written as one string.

      A spinner keys on `Fraction` like a bar does, and ignores the value: it says the work
      is alive, not how far along it is, and draws the same again when there is no value at
      all, which is what makes it the design to import for indeterminate work. The
      `spinners` package holds some forty, and one of your own is one expression:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric
      import palettes.emberGaugePalette

      given blink: (Fraction is Gaugeable) = Spinner.each(t"oO", 300).gaugeable

      gaugeLine(Fraction.indeterminate, 1, Tick.at(300, 300)).plain   // t"O"
      ```
  refer ultimatum.spinners
  refer ultimatum.Fraction
  entry .each   a spinner from single-cell frames written as one string, split by code point
      `Spinner.each(t"⠋⠙⠹⠸⠼⠴⠦⠧⠇⠏", interval = 80, repertoire = Unicode, narrower = Unset)`.
      Splitting by code point rather than by `Char` keeps an astral frame (an emoji clock
      face) as one frame. Frames which are themselves multi-codepoint clusters, or wider
      than one cell, are given explicitly as a `Sequence[Text]` to the constructor.
  entry #frames   the frames, in cycle order
  entry #interval   the milliseconds between frames, which the design reports as its `period`
  entry #columns   the width of every frame
  entry #repertoire   the least adventurous `Gaugeable.Glyphs` which can draw this design
  entry #narrower   the design to fall back to when this one will not fit or is not permitted
  entry #fit   the first design in the fallback chain which fits a width and is permitted, if any
      `fit(width, gauging)`: this spinner if its `columns` fit and its `repertoire` is
      permitted, otherwise `narrower`'s answer, or `Unset` at the end of the chain, in which
      case the row is blank.
  entry #leastColumns   the narrowest width in the chain, which is what the layout is told the gauge needs
  entry #gaugeable   this spinner as a `Fraction is Gaugeable`, under the `Gauging` in scope
      Inelastic, one row, with `interval` as its animation period, and the same frames
      whether the fraction is present or `Unset`. The frame is `frames(tick.index % count)`,
      so a test picks one with `Tick.at(index*interval, interval)`; the glyph is drawn in the
      palette's `fill` role.

entry .Standing   how one unit of work has turned out, or has not yet: `Pending`, `Running`, `Succeeded`, `Failed`, `Warned`, `Skipped`
  detail
      One vocabulary serves a one-cell status marker, a `Step` in a checklist and a colour
      lookup in a palette (`GaugePalette#colorOf`). As a status in its own right, a
      `Standing` draws as a single glyph through a `Standing.Marks` design: the companion's
      default is `standings.tickStanding` (`✓ ✗ ! ‑ ⠋ ·`), and the `standings` package has
      heavier, square, ASCII and word forms, imported by name. Every design carries the
      standing in its *glyph* and not only its colour, so it survives a monochrome terminal,
      a redirected stream and a reader who cannot tell red from green.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric
      import palettes.emberGaugePalette
      import stdios.javaLangSystemStdio

      Out.println(e"${gaugeLine(Standing.Succeeded, 1)} built")
      gaugeLine(Standing.Failed, 4)(using standings.wordStanding).plain   // t"FAIL"
      ```
  refer ultimatum.standings
  refer ultimatum.Step
  entry .Marks   the six glyphs one design uses for the six standings, with a fallback chain
    detail
        `Standing.Marks(succeeded, failed, warned, skipped, running, pending, columns = 1,
        repertoire = Unicode, narrower = Unset)`: the glyph for each standing, how wide they
        all are, the least adventurous `Gaugeable.Glyphs` which can draw them, and the
        design to fall back to when they will not fit or are not permitted. Every given in
        `standings` is a `Marks` made a design with `#gaugeable`.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import textMetrics.uniformMetric
        import palettes.emberGaugePalette

        given boxes: (Standing is Gaugeable) =
          Standing.Marks(t"[x]", t"[!]", t"[?]", t"[-]", t"[>]", t"[ ]", 3).gaugeable

        gaugeLine(Standing.Running, 3).plain   // t"[>]"
        ```
    entry #succeeded   the glyph for `Standing.Succeeded`
    entry #failed   the glyph for `Standing.Failed`
    entry #warned   the glyph for `Standing.Warned`
    entry #skipped   the glyph for `Standing.Skipped`
    entry #running   the glyph for `Standing.Running`
    entry #pending   the glyph for `Standing.Pending`
    entry #columns   the width of every glyph in this set
    entry #repertoire   the least adventurous `Gaugeable.Glyphs` which can draw these marks
    entry #narrower   the marks to fall back to when these will not fit or are not permitted
    entry #apply   the glyph for a standing
    entry #fit   the first marks in the fallback chain which fit a width and are permitted, if any
    entry #leastColumns   the narrowest width in the chain
    entry #draw   one standing as a tinted glyph, padded to a width
        The colour is `GaugePalette#colorOf` for the standing; a width no marks in the chain
        fit gives a blank row.
    entry #gaugeable   these marks as a `Standing is Gaugeable`, inelastic and one row
  entry .gaugeable   the default design, `standings.tickStanding`: the near-universal tick and cross

entry .Step   one named stage of a multi-stage process, with its standing and an optional note
    `Step(name, standing, detail = Unset)`. A run of steps is a plain `Sequence[Step]`, which
    the `processions` givens draw as a checklist, a breadcrumb, a chain of beads, a numbered
    position or a ribbon. The `detail` (an error, an artifact, a duration) is carried for a
    design with room to show it beside the step.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import textMetrics.uniformMetric
    import palettes.emberGaugePalette
    import processions.beadProcession

    val steps: Sequence[Step] =
      Sequence
        ( Step(t"resolve", Standing.Succeeded),
          Step(t"compile", Standing.Running),
          Step(t"publish", Standing.Pending) )

    gaugeLine(steps, 5).plain   // t"●━◐━○"
    ```

entry .Stopwatch   how a duration is written: `Compact` (`2m41s`) or `Digital` (`02:41`)
  detail
      The engine behind the `timers` givens. `Compact` gives the two largest units which
      carry information, through `Magnitude.interval`; `Digital` gives a clock face,
      `mm:ss` growing an hours field only when there are hours, which is wider but does not
      change shape as it crosses a minute. The same two designs serve elapsed time (an
      `aviation.Duration`, through `#elapsed`) and a `Countdown` (through `#countdown`),
      but as separate givens: the two types are distinct precisely so that a countdown can
      redden as it runs out while an elapsed time stays quiet.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric
      import palettes.emberGaugePalette

      gaugeLine(161.0*Second, 5)(using timers.compactElapsed).plain    // t"2m41s"
      gaugeLine(3661.0*Second, 7)(using timers.digitalElapsed).plain   // t"1:01:01"
      gaugeLine(161.0*Second, 3)(using timers.compactElapsed).plain    // t"41s": the seconds survive
      ```

      Neither timer animates: it changes only when its reading does. Too narrow for the
      figure, a timer drops its leading characters, so the seconds — the part which is
      moving — are what survives.
  refer ultimatum.timers
  refer ultimatum.Countdown
  entry #write   a duration in seconds as text, in this style
  entry #columns   the cells the written duration takes
  entry #elapsed   this style as a `Duration is Gaugeable`, drawn in the palette's `muted` role
      Elapsed time is reference material, not a warning. Inelastic and one row.
  entry #countdown   this style as a `Countdown is Gaugeable`, optionally reddening as time runs out
      `countdown(urgent)`: with `urgent` the figure is coloured by `GaugePalette#severity`
      read backwards over the last minute, so it passes through the warning colour and
      reddens as it approaches zero; without, it is drawn in the `caption` role.

entry .Transfer   bytes moved against bytes to move, with the time it has taken
  detail
      `Transfer(moved, total = Unset, elapsed = 0.0*Second)`, with `moved` and `total` as
      `Quantity[Bytes[1]]`. The rate and the estimate are *derived* (`#rate`, `#estimate`),
      never stored, so a caller cannot hand a design an inconsistent triple — the commonest
      bug in hand-rolled transfer displays, where the percentage and the ETA disagree. It
      has no default design: whether to show a rate, whether to show an estimate, and
      whether to count in `MB` or `MiB` are three editorial decisions, so one of the
      `counters` givens (`transferCounter`, `rateTransferCounter`, `terseTransferCounter`,
      `decimalTransferCounter`) is imported by name.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric
      import palettes.emberGaugePalette
      import counters.transferCounter

      val transfer = Transfer(14.2*Mebi(Byte), 512.0*Mebi(Byte), 4.6*Second)
      gaugeLine(transfer, 80)   // 14.2 MiB/512 MiB 3.09 MiB/s 2m41s left
      gaugeLine(transfer, 20)   // the figures alone: the estimate goes first, then the rate
      ```
  refer ultimatum.counters
  refer quantitative.Byte
  entry #moved   the bytes moved so far
  entry #total   the bytes to move, or `Unset` while unknown
  entry #elapsed   the time taken so far
  entry #rate   bytes per second so far; zero before any time has elapsed
  entry #fraction   `moved/total` as a `Fraction`, or `Unset` while the total is unknown
  entry #estimate   how long the rest should take at the rate so far
      `Unset` while nothing has moved (when any estimate would be a fabrication) or while
      the total is unknown.

entry .internal   the opaque status types `Fraction` and `Countdown`, and their companions
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the object exists so that the opaque types have a stable home
      whose abstraction does not leak (a top-level opaque type is lifted into a synthesized
      wrapper, and a type variable inferred from it widens to the representation). Both are
      exported to the `ultimatum` package, and documented under `ultimatum.Fraction` and
      `ultimatum.Countdown`. Each is bounded by `caps.Pure`, so a status is data and may be
      assigned into a `Reading` without the capture checker objecting.
  entry .Fraction   a proportion in `[0, 1]`, clamped; see `ultimatum.Fraction`
    detail
        The opaque type `Fraction <: Matchable & caps.Pure`, over a `Double`, with its
        companion's constructors, default design and reading extensions. Its page under the
        `ultimatum` package has the full account.
    entry .apply   `Fraction(value)`: the value clamped to `[0, 1]`, with `NaN` read as zero
    entry .of   `Fraction.of(done, total)`: `done/total`, or zero when the total is not positive
    entry .indeterminate   work in flight whose total is not known: `Unset`, typed as `Optional[Fraction]`
        Progress is `Optional[Fraction]` rather than a separate "busy" type, so a job which
        starts out unmeasurable and later learns its total does not change type half way
        through, and every design decides both renderings.
    entry .gaugeable   the default design: `bars.smoothBar`, the smooth eighth-block bar
        Every candidate bar is one row and says the same thing, so a default is safe here in
        a way it is not for a meter or a procession. `Optional[Fraction]` needs no separate
        default: `Gaugeable.optional` lifts this one, and the bar's absent rendering sweeps.
    entry .value   the fraction as a `Double` in `[0, 1]`
    entry .percentage   the fraction as a whole-number percentage, truncated
    entry .complete   whether the fraction is 1
  entry .Countdown   time left, clamped at zero; see `ultimatum.Countdown`
    detail
        The opaque type `Countdown <: caps.Pure`, over an `aviation.Duration`. Its page under
        the `ultimatum` package has the full account.
    entry .apply   `Countdown(duration)`: the duration, or zero if it is negative
    entry .gaugeable   the default design: `timers.compactCountdown`
    entry .duration   the remaining time as a `Duration`

entry .bars   eighteen determinate progress bars for `Fraction`, each a given to import by name
  detail
      Which bar draws a `Fraction` is decided by the `Fraction is Gaugeable` given in scope.
      With no import, the companion supplies `smoothBar`; importing one of these by name
      replaces it, and nothing else changes — `import bars.*` would import none of them,
      since a wildcard does not import givens. Every design is a `Bar` value, so width
      negotiation, sub-cell quantization, the loss of the end caps below eight cells and
      the fall through to a percentage at four cells and to a single shade at one are shared
      by all of them.

      ```scala fuel
      alternative Smooth
        code
            import soundness.*
            import textMetrics.uniformMetric
            import palettes.emberGaugePalette

            gaugeLine(Fraction(0.55), 10).plain   // t"█████▌    "
      alternative Block
        code
            locally:
              import bars.blockBar
              gaugeLine(Fraction(0.55), 10).plain   // t"█████░░░░░"
      alternative Arrowhead
        code
            locally:
              import bars.arrowheadBar
              gaugeLine(Fraction(0.55), 10).plain   // t"[====>   ]"
      alternative Segmented
        code
            locally:
              import bars.segmentedBar
              gaugeLine(Fraction(0.55), 10).plain   // t"▰▰▰▰▰▱▱▱▱▱"
      alternative Marker
        code
            locally:
              import bars.markerBar
              gaugeLine(Fraction(0.55), 10).plain   // t"────◆─────"
      alternative Percentage
        code
            locally:
              import bars.percentageBar
              gaugeLine(Fraction(0.55), 10).plain   // t"       55%", seven spaces
      ##
      ```

      A bar is elastic, so in a layout it takes the width it is offered; `Bar.defaultColumns`
      (40) is what it asks for. Two `Fraction` designs in one scope conflict, as do a bar and
      a spinner, so a layout wanting both binds one as a local given.
  refer ultimatum.Bar
  refer ultimatum.Fraction
  entry .smoothBar   the default: full blocks with an eighth-width boundary cell, `█████▌    `
      The boundary cell is one of `▏▎▍▌▋▊▉`, so the bar's edge advances by an eighth of a
      cell at a time; the track is a space, shown as a background in the palette's `track`.
  entry .blockBar   full blocks over a `░` track, stepping cell by cell: `████░░░░`
  entry .shadedBar   full blocks with a `░▒▓` boundary cell over a space track
  entry .risingBar   the boundary cell rises rather than widens: `▁▂▃▄▅▆▇` then `█`
      A different reading of the same fraction, and the one to use when the bar is short.
  entry .fineBar   a heavy rule `━` with a `╸` boundary, over a rule of the same weight in the track colour
  entry .dotBar   `●` over a `·` track, stepping cell by cell
  entry .railBar   a heavy rule `━` with a `╸` boundary, over a light rule `─`
  entry .squareBar   `■` over a `□` track, stepping cell by cell
  entry .brailleBar   braille cells filling dot by dot, `⣿` over a `⣀` track
      Its boundary cell has seven steps (`⡀⡄⡆⡇⣇⣧⣷`), like the smooth bar's, in the braille
      repertoire.
  entry .capsuleBar   the smooth bar between `▕` and `▏` caps, over a `░` track
      The caps mark the extent, so a partly-filled bar in a wide column still reads as a
      proportion of something; they are the first cells given up when the column narrows.
  entry .asciiBar   `[####----]`: seven-bit, with caps
      Below eight cells the caps go and it still fills: `###---` at six.
  entry .equalsBar   `[====    ]`: seven-bit, with caps and a space track
  entry .arrowheadBar   the classic `[===>    ]`, with the tip at the head of the fill
      The arrowhead is a `Bar.Glyphs#tip` rather than a partial: it marks where the fill has
      reached at every intermediate value, drawn in the palette's `leadingEdge`, and is
      absent only when the bar is empty or full.
  entry .gradientBar   the smooth bar with each cell coloured by its position along the bar
      The fill colour is taken per cell from `GaugePalette#lengthwise`, so the bar carries a
      gradient along its length rather than one flat colour.
  entry .segmentedBar   twenty pips, `▰` lit and `▱` hollow, with no gap between them
      Coarser than a smooth bar, but legible at a glance and countable. In a narrower
      column fewer pips are drawn rather than clipped.
  entry .pipBar   ten pips, `●` lit and `○` hollow, separated by spaces
  entry .markerBar   a `◆` head travelling along a `─` rail, with nothing filled behind it
      A position rather than an amount, for something which scrubs back and forth.
  entry .percentageBar   the figure alone, right-aligned: `  7%`, `100%`
      For a column with no room for anything else, and the design every other bar degrades
      into below four cells. Four cells is its preferred width.

entry .spinners   some forty indeterminate activity indicators for `Fraction`, each a given to import by name
  detail
      A spinner keys on `Fraction`, like a bar, and ignores the value: it says the work is
      alive, not how far along it is, and draws its frames whether the fraction is present
      or `Unset`. Importing one by name replaces the bar for every `Fraction` gauge in
      scope; there is no default spinner, since the default `Fraction` design is the bar,
      and a layout wanting both a bar and a spinner binds one as a local given:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric
      import palettes.emberGaugePalette

      val resolving: Tui.Pane =
        given spinner: (Fraction is Gaugeable) = spinners.brailleDotsSpinner
        gauge(Reading(Fraction.indeterminate))

      val copying: Tui.Pane = gauge(Reading(Fraction(0.0)))
      ```

      Every design names the least adventurous `Gaugeable.Glyphs` which can draw it and,
      where it is wide or exotic, what to fall back to, so the catalogue degrades as a whole
      under `gaugeGlyphs.asciiGlyphs` and in a narrow column without the caller choosing a
      different design: an emoji design becomes its BMP sibling where the terminal has not
      said it renders emoji, a multi-cell marquee becomes a single spinning cell at one
      column, and under ASCII every spinner emits seven-bit output.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label Braille dots, frame by frame: one frame every 80 ms
        code
            import soundness.*
            import textMetrics.uniformMetric
            import palettes.emberGaugePalette
            import spinners.brailleDotsSpinner

            gaugeLine(Fraction.indeterminate, 1, Tick.at(0, 80)).plain     // t"⠋"
            gaugeLine(Fraction.indeterminate, 1, Tick.at(80, 80)).plain    // t"⠙"
            gaugeLine(Fraction.indeterminate, 1, Tick.at(160, 80)).plain   // t"⠹"
      frame
        label A six-cell marquee at its full width
        code
            locally:
              import spinners.bouncingBarSpinner
              gaugeLine(Fraction.indeterminate, 6, Tick.at(80, 80)).plain   // t"[=   ]"
      frame
        label The same marquee in a one-cell column falls back to the line spinner
        code
            locally:
              import spinners.bouncingBarSpinner
              gaugeLine(Fraction.indeterminate, 1, Tick.at(80, 80)).plain   // t"\\"
      frame
        label Moon phases where emoji are permitted, and circles where they are not
        code
            locally:
              import spinners.moonPhaseSpinner
              import gaugeGlyphs.emojiGlyphs
              gaugeLine(Fraction.indeterminate, 2, Tick.zero).plain   // t"🌑"

            locally:
              import spinners.moonPhaseSpinner
              gaugeLine(Fraction.indeterminate, 2, Tick.zero).plain   // t"◌ "
      ##
      ```

      Each spinner's period is its frame interval, and a conducted layout runs one timer at
      the shortest period over the gauges on screen.
  refer ultimatum.Spinner
  refer ultimatum.Fraction
  refer ultimatum.gaugeGlyphs
  entry .lineSpinner   the ASCII `-\|/` line, at 130 ms: the end of most fallback chains
  entry .crossStarSpinner   `+x*`, ASCII, at 120 ms
  entry .dqpbSpinner   `dqpb`, a rotating letter, ASCII, at 100 ms
  entry .bounceSpinner   `.oO°Oo.`, a ball swelling and shrinking, ASCII, at 120 ms
  entry .balloonSpinner   ` .oO@* `, a balloon inflating and bursting, ASCII, at 140 ms
  entry .brailleDotsSpinner   `⠋⠙⠹⠸⠼⠴⠦⠧⠇⠏`, the standard braille dots, at 80 ms
      The densest single-cell animation there is, and the one most terminals show best.
      Falls back to `lineSpinner` under ASCII.
  entry .brailleSnakeSpinner   `⣾⣽⣻⢿⡿⣟⣯⣷`, a gap chasing round a full braille cell, at 80 ms
  entry .brailleWaveSpinner   `⠁⠂⠄⡀⢀⠠⠐⠈`, a single dot circling the cell, at 90 ms
  entry .brailleGrowSpinner   `⠋⠙⠚⠞⠖⠦⠴⠲⠳⠓`, braille dots growing and shrinking, at 80 ms
  entry .brailleRingSpinner   `⣸⢹⠻⠟⡏⣇⣦⣴`, a ring of six dots with a two-dot gap chasing round it, at 80 ms
  entry .arcSpinner   `◜◠◝◞◡◟`, an arc circling, at 100 ms
  entry .circleQuadrantSpinner   `◴◷◶◵`, a quadrant circling, at 120 ms
      Also the fallback of `clockSpinner` where emoji are not permitted.
  entry .circleHalfSpinner   `◐◓◑◒`, a half-circle rotating, at 120 ms
      Also the fallback of `earthSpinner`.
  entry .circlePulseSpinner   `◌○◎◉●◉◎○`, a circle filling and emptying, at 120 ms
      Also the fallback of `moonPhaseSpinner`.
  entry .squareCornerSpinner   `◰◳◲◱`, a quadrant of a square circling, at 120 ms
  entry .triangleSpinner   `◢◣◤◥`, a triangle rotating, at 120 ms
  entry .pipeSpinner   `┤┘┴└├┌┬┐`, a box-drawing corner rotating, at 100 ms
  entry .boxSpinner   `▖▘▝▗`, a quadrant block circling, at 120 ms
  entry .arrowSpinner   `←↖↑↗→↘↓↙`, an arrow turning through the compass, at 100 ms
  entry .arrowDoubleSpinner   `⇐⇖⇑⇗⇒⇘⇓⇙`, a double arrow turning, at 100 ms
  entry .starSpinner   `✶✸✹✺✹✷`, a star twinkling, at 70 ms
  entry .hamburgerSpinner   `☱☲☴`, trigram lines shifting, at 100 ms
  entry .noiseSpinner   `▓▒░`, shading cycling, at 100 ms
      An amount of ink rather than a shape, so it reads as effort.
  entry .pulseSpinner   `█▓▒░▒▓`, a cell pulsing between full and light, at 100 ms
  entry .growingBarSpinner   `▏▎▍▌▋▊▉█▉▊▋▌▍▎`, a cell widening and narrowing, at 90 ms
  entry .growingBlockSpinner   `▁▃▄▅▆▇█▇▆▅▄▃`, a cell rising and falling, at 90 ms
  entry .layerSpinner   `-=≡`, lines stacking, ASCII, at 150 ms
  entry .toggleSpinner   `⊶⊷`, two states, at 250 ms
      A toggle is slow and has two states, for something which is alive rather than working.
  entry .toggleSquareSpinner   `▫▪`, a square toggling, at 250 ms
  entry .toggleRoundSpinner   `⦾⦿`, a circle toggling, at 250 ms
  entry .hourglassThinSpinner   `⧖⧗`, a BMP hourglass turning, at 500 ms
      One of the two single-cell designs which say "waiting" rather than "working"; also
      the fallback of `hourglassSpinner`.
  entry .pointsSpinner   `∙∙∙` with one `●` travelling along it, three cells, at 125 ms
      Falls back to `lineSpinner` in a column narrower than three cells.
  entry .dotsScrollSpinner   `.  `, `.. `, `...`: dots accumulating, three cells, ASCII, at 200 ms
  entry .binarySpinner   six cells of changing binary digits, ASCII, at 80 ms
  entry .bouncingBarSpinner   `[=   ]` to `[====]` and back, six cells, ASCII, at 80 ms
  entry .bouncingBallSpinner   `( ●    )`, a ball bouncing between brackets, eight cells, at 80 ms
  entry .aestheticSpinner   `▰▰▰▱▱▱▱`, seven pips filling then clearing, at 120 ms
  entry .shuttleSpinner   `▸▹▹▹▹`, a filled arrowhead travelling along five hollow ones, at 120 ms
  entry .clockSpinner   emoji clock faces `🕛🕐🕑…`, two cells, at 100 ms
      Only under `gaugeGlyphs.emojiGlyphs`; otherwise `circleQuadrantSpinner`.
  entry .moonPhaseSpinner   emoji moon phases `🌑🌒🌓🌔🌕🌖🌗🌘`, two cells, at 80 ms
      Only under `gaugeGlyphs.emojiGlyphs`; otherwise `circlePulseSpinner`.
  entry .earthSpinner   the emoji globe `🌍🌎🌏` turning, two cells, at 180 ms
      Only under `gaugeGlyphs.emojiGlyphs`; otherwise `circleHalfSpinner`.
  entry .hourglassSpinner   the emoji hourglass `⏳⌛`, two cells, at 500 ms
      Only under `gaugeGlyphs.emojiGlyphs`; otherwise `hourglassThinSpinner`.

entry .meters   six designs for a `Meter`, each a given to import by name; there is no default
  detail
      A meter is a reading on a bounded scale which can fall as well as rise, so these
      designs mark the scale and colour the reading by where it sits on it, through
      `GaugePalette#severity`. `Meter` has no default design: a battery, a thermometer and
      a bullet chart are not substitutable, and two of them are not even the same height,
      so one is always imported by name.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric
      import palettes.emberGaugePalette
      import meters.needleMeter

      gaugeLine(Meter(0.5), 12)
      ```
  refer ultimatum.Meter
  refer ultimatum.Dial
  entry .batteryMeter   capped cells with a terminal nub, `▐███░░░░▌╸`, reddening as it empties
      Eight cells preferred; `[####----]` under ASCII. The severity ramp is read backwards,
      since a battery is in danger when it is low.
  entry .thermometerMeter   a five-row column read from the bottom up, with a bulb `◍` beneath
      One cell wide and inelastic; the top filled cell is a partial block, so the reading
      moves smoothly rather than a fifth at a time.
  entry .needleMeter   a tick `┃` travelling along a `─` scale with marked ends
      Twelve cells preferred. A position on a range, with no suggestion that the space
      behind it has been filled.
  entry .bulletMeter   a measure over a qualitative track whose `░▒▓` bands mark low, fair and high
      Twenty cells preferred; the bands make the reading interpretable without a legend.
  entry .columnMeter   a single cell whose block height `▁`…`█` is the reading
      One cell wide and inelastic: `Meter(0.5)` is `▅`.
  entry .asciiMeter   `[####----]`, seven-bit, with the fill coloured by severity

entry .sparklines   four designs for a `Sequence[Double]`, each a given to import by name; there is no default
  detail
      A run of samples drawn as a miniature chart, one cell per sample, scaled to the range
      of the samples it is given (or to fixed bounds, through `Sparkline#scaled`). A
      sparkline has no default design, because the choice sets the vertical resolution —
      eight levels or four, one row or two — and that changes what the reader can see in
      the data. A sparkline narrower than its series is decimated, keeping each group's
      peak, never truncated.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric
      import palettes.emberGaugePalette
      import sparklines.blockSparkline

      gaugeLine(Sequence(1.0, 3.0, 2.0, 5.0, 4.0), 5).plain   // t"▁▅▃█▇"
      ```
  refer ultimatum.Sparkline
  entry .blockSparkline   eight levels in one row, `▁▂▃▄▅▆▇█`
  entry .tallSparkline   sixteen levels over two rows: the upper row the top half of the range, the lower the bottom
      Resolves detail a single row would flatten; collapses to one ASCII row under
      `gaugeGlyphs.asciiGlyphs`.
  entry .dotSparkline   four levels of braille dots, `⣀⠤⠒⠉`
  entry .asciiSparkline   four levels in seven-bit characters, `_.-^`
      Also what every other sparkline falls back to under `gaugeGlyphs.asciiGlyphs`.

entry .counters   figures rather than pictures: five counters for a `Reckoning` and four reports for a `Transfer`
  detail
      Each is a given to import by name. `Reckoning` has a default (`plainCounter`, since
      `17/120` is not a style question); `Transfer` deliberately does not, since choosing a
      report is also choosing whether to show a rate and an estimate, and whether to count
      in `MiB` or `MB`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric
      import palettes.emberGaugePalette
      import counters.paddedCounter
      import counters.transferCounter

      gaugeLine(Reckoning(7, 120), 7).plain   // t"  7/120"
      gaugeLine(Transfer(14.2*Mebi(Byte), 512.0*Mebi(Byte), 4.6*Second), 44)
      ```

      A transfer report is a row of `Facet`s — the moved/total figures, the rate and the
      estimate — shed in descending order of what a reader can do without: at a narrow width
      the estimate goes first, then the rate, leaving the figures.
  refer ultimatum.Reckoning
  refer ultimatum.Transfer
  refer ultimatum.Magnitude
  entry .plainCounter   `17/120`, or `17` while the total is unknown: the default
  entry .paddedCounter   `  7/120`: the numerator right-aligned to the total's digit count
      The field does not jitter as it counts up — the difference between a figure which
      can be read while it changes and one which cannot.
  entry .wordCounter   `17 of 120`
  entry .scaledCounter   `1.2k/8.4k`: both figures abbreviated through `Magnitude.count`
  entry .percentageCounter   `  7%`…`100%` from the reckoning's fraction, or `  ?%` with no total
  entry .transferCounter   figures, rate and estimate in binary prefixes: `14.2 MiB/512 MiB 3.09 MiB/s 2m41s left`
      Binary prefixes (`KiB`, `MiB`) are the convention for anything counted in blocks on a
      disk or a wire.
  entry .rateTransferCounter   figures and rate in binary prefixes, with no estimate
  entry .terseTransferCounter   the figures alone, in binary prefixes
  entry .decimalTransferCounter   figures, rate and estimate in decimal prefixes: `4.20 MB`
      Decimal prefixes (`kB`, `MB`) are the convention a drive manufacturer and a network
      operator use.

entry .standings   five one-cell (or one-word) markers for a `Standing`, each a given to import by name
  detail
      With no import, `Standing`'s companion supplies `tickStanding`. Every design carries
      the standing in its *glyph*, not only in its colour, so it survives a monochrome
      terminal, a redirected stream and a reader who cannot distinguish red from green; and
      every Unicode design falls back to `asciiStanding` under `gaugeGlyphs.asciiGlyphs`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric
      import palettes.emberGaugePalette
      import standings.heavyStanding

      gaugeLine(Standing.Succeeded, 1).plain   // t"✔"
      gaugeLine(Standing.Warned, 1).plain      // t"⚠"
      ```
  refer ultimatum.Standing
  entry .tickStanding   `✓ ✗ ! ‑ ⠋ ·` for succeeded, failed, warned, skipped, running and pending: the default
  entry .heavyStanding   `✔ ✘ ⚠ ⊘ ◐ ◌`
  entry .squareStanding   `■ ▨ ▩ □ ▤ ·`
  entry .asciiStanding   `+ x ! - * .`: seven-bit, and the fallback of every other design
  entry .wordStanding   `  ok`, `FAIL`, `warn`, `skip`, ` run`, `   …`: four cells each, so a column aligns
      Words rather than glyphs, for a transcript which will be read rather than watched.

entry .processions   five designs for a run of steps, a `Sequence[Step]`, each a given to import by name
  detail
      A run of steps has no default design, because these differ in height — a checklist
      is one row per step where the rest are a single row — and choosing one decides how
      much of the layout the gauge takes, which is not a decision a library should make
      silently. Each step's marker and colour come from the same `Standing` vocabulary a
      one-cell status glyph uses.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label A checklist: one row per step, the running one animated
        code
            import soundness.*
            import textMetrics.uniformMetric
            import palettes.emberGaugePalette

            val steps: Sequence[Step] =
              Sequence
                ( Step(t"resolve", Standing.Succeeded),
                  Step(t"compile", Standing.Running),
                  Step(t"publish", Standing.Pending) )

            locally:
              import processions.checklistProcession
              gaugeRows(steps, 9).map(_.plain)   // List(t"✓ resolve", t"⠋ compile", t"· publish")
      frame
        label A numbered position: the running step's name and how many have started
        code
            locally:
              import processions.numberedProcession
              gaugeLine(steps, 14).plain   // t"[2/3] compile "
      frame
        label Beads: one per step, joined by a rule
        code
            locally:
              import processions.beadProcession
              gaugeLine(steps, 5).plain   // t"●━◐━○"
      frame
        label A breadcrumb: every step's name on one row
        code
            locally:
              import processions.breadcrumbProcession
              gaugeLine(steps, 29).plain   // t"resolve › compile › publish  "
      ##
      ```
  refer ultimatum.Checklist
  refer ultimatum.Step
  refer ultimatum.Standing
  entry .checklistProcession   one row per step, marked `✓ ✗ ! ‑ ·` by standing, with the running step's marker spinning
      Three cells minimum, as tall as the sequence; its 80 ms period animates the running
      step's braille marker (`-\|/` under ASCII). Succeeded and skipped steps are faded to
      the palette's `muted`.
  entry .breadcrumbProcession   the step names on one row, joined by `›`, pending steps faded to the track colour
  entry .beadProcession   a chain of one bead per step (`○` pending, `◐` running, `●` done) joined by `━`
      Fixed at `2n - 1` cells for `n` steps; animates at 80 ms.
  entry .numberedProcession   `[2/3] compile`: how many steps have started, and the running step's name
  entry .ribbonProcession   a powerline ribbon of coloured segments, one per step
      The backgrounds come from `GaugePalette#steps`. It renders as tofu without a patched
      font, and no `Termcap` can say whether one is installed, so it is opt-in and never a
      default.

entry .palettes   ten colour palettes for gauges, each a given to import by name
  detail
      Which colours a gauge draws in is an axis entirely separate from which design it uses,
      so swapping either is one import and neither disturbs the other. With no import,
      `GaugePalette.adaptive` picks `monochromeGaugePalette`, `ansiSixteenGaugePalette` or
      `slateGaugePalette` from the terminal's reported colour depth; naming one asserts that
      you know what your terminal does. The members are suffixed `GaugePalette` because this
      block merges with the umbrella's `palettes` block from `iridescence`, so every name
      must be distinct across the whole of `soundness`.

      ```scala fuel
      alternative Ember
        code
            import soundness.*
            import textMetrics.uniformMetric

            locally:
              import palettes.emberGaugePalette
              gaugeLine(Fraction(0.6), 20)
      alternative Solarized dark
        code
            locally:
              import palettes.solarizedDarkGaugePalette
              gaugeLine(Fraction(0.6), 20)
      alternative Monochrome
        code
            locally:
              import palettes.monochromeGaugePalette
              gaugeLine(Fraction(0.6), 20)
      ##
      ```
  refer ultimatum.GaugePalette
  entry .monochromeGaugePalette   hue-free: the terminal's two colours and half-tones between them
      The only palette which is honestly correct on a monochrome terminal, and the one to
      reach for when a gauge's output may be read as text; the adaptive default for a
      terminal reporting no colour or eight colours.
  entry .ansiSixteenGaugePalette   only the colours a sixteen-colour terminal actually has
      Each is that terminal's canonical value, so even the 256-cube approximation lands on a
      shade it can display rather than between two; the adaptive default for sixteen
      colours.
  entry .solarizedDarkGaugePalette   Solarized dark, with its blue fill and cyan leading edge
  entry .solarizedLightGaugePalette   Solarized light, on its cream background
  entry .emberGaugePalette   the house palette: orange fill on deep brown, what a Soundness progress bar has always looked like
  entry .oceanicGaugePalette   cool teal and low contrast, for a gauge which will sit on screen for hours
  entry .verdantGaugePalette   greens on a dark green ground
  entry .plumGaugePalette   violets on a dark plum ground
  entry .slateGaugePalette   cool greys with one blue accent: the fill is the only saturated colour on screen
      The eye goes to the progress and nothing else; the adaptive default for a true-colour
      or 256-colour terminal.
  entry .signalGaugePalette   maximum separation between the success, warning and danger colours, for CI logs read at a glance

entry .timers   durations written out: two designs for elapsed time and three for a `Countdown`
  detail
      Elapsed time is an `aviation.Duration`; a `Countdown` keeps its own type so that the
      two can be shown side by side with different designs, and so a countdown can signal
      urgency as it runs out. `Countdown`'s companion defaults to `compactCountdown`; a
      `Duration` has no default design here, so one of `compactElapsed` or `digitalElapsed`
      is imported by name. Neither animates: a timer changes only when its reading does.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric
      import palettes.emberGaugePalette
      import timers.digitalElapsed
      import timers.urgentCountdown

      gaugeLine(61.0*Second, 5).plain              // t"01:01"
      gaugeLine(Countdown(45.0*Second), 3).plain   // t"45s", reddening
      ```
  refer ultimatum.Stopwatch
  refer ultimatum.Countdown
  entry .compactElapsed   elapsed time as its two largest useful units: `41s`, `2m41s`, `1h04m`
  entry .digitalElapsed   elapsed time as a clock face: `00:41`, `02:41`, `1:01:01`
      Wider than the compact form, but does not change shape as it crosses a minute.
  entry .compactCountdown   time left as `2m41s`, in the caption colour: the default
  entry .digitalCountdown   time left as a clock face, `02:41`
  entry .urgentCountdown   time left as `2m41s`, reddening through the severity ramp over the last minute
      Under a minute is where a countdown starts to matter; anything longer sits at the
      calm end of the ramp.

entry .captions   where a label sits relative to the gauge it labels, each a given to import by name
  detail
      A `CaptionLayout` for `Captioned` statuses, orthogonal to which design is used and to
      which palette colours it. With no import, `CaptionLayout`'s companion supplies the
      trailing layout.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric
      import palettes.emberGaugePalette
      import bars.blockBar
      import captions.leadingCaption

      gaugeLine(Captioned(Fraction(0.5), t"go"), 8).plain   // t"go ██░░░"
      ```
  refer ultimatum.CaptionLayout
  refer ultimatum.Captioned
  entry .trailingCaption   the label follows the gauge, one space away, elided when long: the default
  entry .leadingCaption   the label precedes the gauge, one space away, elided when long
  entry .spacedCaption   the label follows the gauge, two spaces away
  entry .truncatedCaption   never shorten the label, even where that leaves the gauge less room than it wants
      For a caption whose exact text matters more than the bar beside it.

entry .gaugeGlyphs   which characters the designs may use, each a given to import by name
  detail
      A `Gaugeable.Glyphs` repertoire. Importing one degrades or promotes the whole
      catalogue at once, rather than requiring a different design to be chosen for each
      gauge: every design has an ASCII rendering, and every design which prefers exotic
      glyphs declares what to fall back to. With no import, the companion's default is
      `Unicode`.

      ```scala fuel
      alternative Unicode
        code
            import soundness.*
            import textMetrics.uniformMetric
            import palettes.emberGaugePalette
            import spinners.brailleDotsSpinner

            gaugeLine(Fraction.indeterminate, 1).plain   // t"⠋"
            gaugeLine(Standing.Succeeded, 1).plain       // t"✓"
      alternative ASCII
        code
            locally:
              import gaugeGlyphs.asciiGlyphs
              gaugeLine(Fraction.indeterminate, 1).plain   // t"-"
              gaugeLine(Standing.Succeeded, 1).plain       // t"+"
      ##
      ```
  refer ultimatum.Gaugeable
  entry .asciiGlyphs   seven-bit output only: safe in a log capture, a CI transcript, or a terminal whose font is unknown
      Every design has an ASCII rendering, so nothing disappears: bars become `[###---]`,
      spinners `-\|/`, standings `+ x ! - * .`, sparklines `_.-^`.
  entry .unicodeGlyphs   box-drawing and block glyphs: the default, and universal on modern terminals
  entry .brailleGlyphs   also braille, which packs two to four samples into a cell but needs a font which has it
  entry .emojiGlyphs   also emoji, which are two cells wide and need a terminal which agrees they are
      Enables `spinners.clockSpinner`, `moonPhaseSpinner`, `earthSpinner` and
      `hourglassSpinner`; without it each draws its BMP sibling.

entry .informationPrefixes   how a byte quantity is scaled for display: binary or decimal prefixes, each a given to import by name
  detail
      A `Prefixes on Bytes[1]` for rendering a `Quantity[Bytes[1]]` through `quantitative`.
      Neither is a default: `1.5 MB` and `1.43 MiB` are the same quantity written under
      different conventions, and which is right depends on what is being measured, so the
      caller picks.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import informationPrefixes.binaryBytes

      val size: Quantity[Bytes[1]] = 1500000.0*Byte
      ```
  refer quantitative.Byte
  refer quantitative.Bytes
  entry .binaryBytes   the 1024-based prefixes `Ki`, `Mi`, `Gi`, `Ti`: the convention for blocks on a disk or a wire
  entry .decimalBytes   the 1000-based prefixes `k`, `M`, `G`, `T`: the convention of drive manufacturers and network operators
